WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.968 --> 00:00:02.904
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:00:59.700 --> 00:01:02.670
PRESENTER: This is the first in
a series of lectures sponsored

00:01:02.670 --> 00:01:04.590
by the Whitehead Institute.

00:01:07.530 --> 00:01:12.630
It arose because there
are now new techniques

00:01:12.630 --> 00:01:16.630
in molecular biology
that have made

00:01:16.630 --> 00:01:22.140
it possible to diagnose
hidden genetic disorders,

00:01:22.140 --> 00:01:25.480
redesign farm animals,
produce plants

00:01:25.480 --> 00:01:30.320
with new and unusual
characteristics.

00:01:30.320 --> 00:01:35.450
It's clear that these techniques
will lead to new approaches

00:01:35.450 --> 00:01:39.800
to problems outside
of the laboratory--

00:01:39.800 --> 00:01:42.800
hunger, environmental
pollution, and disease.

00:01:42.800 --> 00:01:47.460
But they also raise important
social and ethical questions.

00:01:47.460 --> 00:01:49.940
For example, who
will have access

00:01:49.940 --> 00:01:53.330
to the results of genetic tests?

00:01:53.330 --> 00:01:55.330
What does it mean to
patent a new life form?

00:01:57.880 --> 00:02:01.420
How will new technologies
affect the likelihood

00:02:01.420 --> 00:02:02.560
of biological warfare?

00:02:06.853 --> 00:02:12.450
The discussions
at the Institute,

00:02:12.450 --> 00:02:17.220
and specifically between
Eric Lander and myself,

00:02:17.220 --> 00:02:23.210
led us to organize
this series, which is

00:02:23.210 --> 00:02:25.640
titled "Biology in the Future."

00:02:25.640 --> 00:02:29.010
Basically to explore
the social, ethical,

00:02:29.010 --> 00:02:31.490
and environmental
consequences of the revolution

00:02:31.490 --> 00:02:32.520
in molecular biology.

00:02:35.460 --> 00:02:37.140
It's supported by
a generous grant

00:02:37.140 --> 00:02:40.440
from the Alfred P.
Sloan Foundation,

00:02:40.440 --> 00:02:44.490
with the specific charge
that we not limit this solely

00:02:44.490 --> 00:02:52.980
to biologists, for reasons that
perhaps are apparent to you.

00:02:52.980 --> 00:02:54.900
And so we have made
every attempt--

00:02:54.900 --> 00:02:57.320
and I believe have succeeded--

00:02:57.320 --> 00:03:02.570
in attracting people from
not only outside the biology

00:03:02.570 --> 00:03:07.710
community, outside of
MIT, to get scientists

00:03:07.710 --> 00:03:11.160
and non-scientists
here to talk about some

00:03:11.160 --> 00:03:17.250
of these possibilities
and what lies ahead.

00:03:17.250 --> 00:03:22.720
Tonight's lecture is
the first of these.

00:03:22.720 --> 00:03:27.410
On March 2 there will
be a lecture, Genetics

00:03:27.410 --> 00:03:30.230
and your Health, Tales of
Genetic Discrimination,

00:03:30.230 --> 00:03:31.190
by Paul Billings.

00:03:33.830 --> 00:03:37.800
On April 29, The
Blood of our Children,

00:03:37.800 --> 00:03:41.000
Genetic Identification
of the Disappeared,

00:03:41.000 --> 00:03:44.390
by Mary Claire King.

00:03:44.390 --> 00:03:47.580
And on September
30, Purple Cows,

00:03:47.580 --> 00:03:51.135
Issues in Biotechnology and
Agriculture, by Dan Kevles.

00:03:54.280 --> 00:03:58.330
And then Genetic Diagnostics,
Reading the Future,

00:03:58.330 --> 00:04:01.340
on November 4.

00:04:01.340 --> 00:04:03.600
The format of this
lecture is that we

00:04:03.600 --> 00:04:10.070
will have first a
presentation by Doctor Matthew

00:04:10.070 --> 00:04:15.200
Meselson, and then some
subsequent comments

00:04:15.200 --> 00:04:17.450
by John Deutch.

00:04:21.320 --> 00:04:30.530
Tonight's speaker on the
future of biological warfare

00:04:30.530 --> 00:04:34.580
is by Matthew Meselson, who
is the Thomas Dudley Cabot

00:04:34.580 --> 00:04:40.730
Professor of the Natural
Sciences at Harvard University.

00:04:40.730 --> 00:04:46.400
I first learned of Matt
from my genetics textbook,

00:04:46.400 --> 00:04:50.150
since he had
performed what is now

00:04:50.150 --> 00:04:55.460
a classical experiment
in molecular biology,

00:04:55.460 --> 00:05:00.260
and one that we still teach to
graduate students as the way

00:05:00.260 --> 00:05:01.760
experiments ought to be done.

00:05:05.900 --> 00:05:10.900
But Matt has, as many
of you know, broadened--

00:05:10.900 --> 00:05:15.350
or, has a broad interest in
matters of public policy,

00:05:15.350 --> 00:05:17.860
and as a consultant to the
Arms Control and Disarmament

00:05:17.860 --> 00:05:21.630
Agency in the 1960s he
helped shape US policy

00:05:21.630 --> 00:05:24.860
on biological and
chemical weapons,

00:05:24.860 --> 00:05:28.310
and he is credited with being
a key influence in President

00:05:28.310 --> 00:05:35.960
Nixon's 1969 decision to
renounce biological warfare

00:05:35.960 --> 00:05:41.020
and to get rid of
the US stockpile

00:05:41.020 --> 00:05:44.680
of microbiological weapons.

00:05:47.780 --> 00:05:51.881
I have always found Matt to
be a compelling and articulate

00:05:51.881 --> 00:05:54.130
speaker, and look forward
to hearing him this evening.

00:05:57.240 --> 00:05:59.042
Would you please welcome
Matthew Meselson.

00:05:59.042 --> 00:06:01.026
[APPLAUSE]

00:06:07.564 --> 00:06:09.730
MATTHEW MESELSON: You have
a very obedient audience.

00:06:21.780 --> 00:06:24.400
The reason I accepted
this invitation

00:06:24.400 --> 00:06:25.704
was that I was invited.

00:06:25.704 --> 00:06:27.400
[LAUGHTER]

00:06:27.400 --> 00:06:32.620
But if I had to justify it
I would say that many of you

00:06:32.620 --> 00:06:34.830
are biologists, I'm sure--

00:06:34.830 --> 00:06:36.430
there being such a
high concentration

00:06:36.430 --> 00:06:39.130
of biologists in this area--

00:06:39.130 --> 00:06:42.010
and may want to get some
information about a subject

00:06:42.010 --> 00:06:44.290
that you don't
deal with everyday,

00:06:44.290 --> 00:06:49.710
but which is still related to
your work one way or another.

00:06:49.710 --> 00:06:54.800
And then, more generally,
people in this community

00:06:54.800 --> 00:06:57.650
must know that it is one of
the few places in the world

00:06:57.650 --> 00:07:01.562
in which the biological
revolution is taking place,

00:07:01.562 --> 00:07:03.020
and are probably
interested to know

00:07:03.020 --> 00:07:06.120
what's going on in your
community in the broadest way.

00:07:06.120 --> 00:07:10.580
So what I'm going to try
to do is simply describe

00:07:10.580 --> 00:07:14.960
some of the history as well as
present issues in this subject,

00:07:14.960 --> 00:07:18.020
and try to be as
informative as I can.

00:07:21.130 --> 00:07:23.230
Why should we be
concerned with this issue

00:07:23.230 --> 00:07:28.900
of the utilization of biological
sciences for hostile purposes?

00:07:28.900 --> 00:07:31.000
Certainly at the outset
one has to realize

00:07:31.000 --> 00:07:34.990
that the implications of the
new biology for human benefit

00:07:34.990 --> 00:07:37.620
are vast.

00:07:37.620 --> 00:07:40.500
But it is also
true that if there

00:07:40.500 --> 00:07:44.280
should come into play
large-scale or serious

00:07:44.280 --> 00:07:48.570
exploitation, deliberate
exploitation of biology

00:07:48.570 --> 00:07:53.730
for hostile purposes, that
this could cause great harm.

00:07:53.730 --> 00:07:57.870
Over the short-run the kind of
harm that's easy to envisage

00:07:57.870 --> 00:08:02.520
and describe in detail
derives from the fact that,

00:08:02.520 --> 00:08:05.400
at least in principle-- though
no one's ever done it and maybe

00:08:05.400 --> 00:08:07.320
an attempt to do
it would fizzle--

00:08:07.320 --> 00:08:11.760
but nevertheless, potentially
with biological weapons

00:08:11.760 --> 00:08:14.940
based on infectious
organisms it could

00:08:14.940 --> 00:08:19.110
be possible to cause illness
and death over very large areas

00:08:19.110 --> 00:08:21.720
with relatively small payloads.

00:08:21.720 --> 00:08:26.070
The reason for this is simply
that the mass, the weight,

00:08:26.070 --> 00:08:29.160
of infectious agent
that is required

00:08:29.160 --> 00:08:32.210
to initiate an infection
in a human being

00:08:32.210 --> 00:08:33.240
is a very small mass.

00:08:36.669 --> 00:08:39.530
Any one number is
going to be misleading,

00:08:39.530 --> 00:08:42.140
because there are so
many variables, starting

00:08:42.140 --> 00:08:44.030
with how many
organisms are required

00:08:44.030 --> 00:08:48.370
to initiate an infection,
say, by the aerosol route

00:08:48.370 --> 00:08:49.460
by inhalation.

00:08:49.460 --> 00:08:52.460
There is great
variation in this number

00:08:52.460 --> 00:08:56.280
depending on the
organism in question.

00:08:56.280 --> 00:08:58.640
Even with a fairly
homogeneous population.

00:08:58.640 --> 00:09:02.360
For example, just
a very few cells

00:09:02.360 --> 00:09:06.120
of the organism that causes
tularemia, Francisella

00:09:06.120 --> 00:09:09.170
tularensis, three,
four, five cells

00:09:09.170 --> 00:09:12.560
probably are enough to
initiate an infection.

00:09:12.560 --> 00:09:15.890
Whereas with another
organism, bacillus anthracis,

00:09:15.890 --> 00:09:20.960
the agent of anthrax, maybe
10, maybe 50,000 spores

00:09:20.960 --> 00:09:24.560
are required before
there's a 50% likelihood

00:09:24.560 --> 00:09:27.039
of infecting a primate.

00:09:27.039 --> 00:09:28.580
We don't do
experiments-- experiments

00:09:28.580 --> 00:09:30.950
like that haven't been
done on human beings

00:09:30.950 --> 00:09:34.130
and recorded, or known to me.

00:09:34.130 --> 00:09:35.510
So there is variation there.

00:09:35.510 --> 00:09:37.520
There are many
other uncertainties.

00:09:37.520 --> 00:09:42.950
Stability of the organism,
susceptibility of a population,

00:09:42.950 --> 00:09:44.780
distribution modalities.

00:09:44.780 --> 00:09:47.680
So if any one number is
unlikely to mean much.

00:09:47.680 --> 00:09:52.190
Nevertheless, even if one allows
for all the variabilities,

00:09:52.190 --> 00:09:56.540
it's pretty clear that
relatively small quantities.

00:09:56.540 --> 00:10:02.310
Some have said maybe as
small a weight per area

00:10:02.310 --> 00:10:06.510
made lethal to be as,
say, hydrogen bombs.

00:10:06.510 --> 00:10:09.910
Maybe a greater weight, maybe
a smaller weight, but perhaps

00:10:09.910 --> 00:10:11.080
in that ballpark.

00:10:11.080 --> 00:10:13.630
Certainly much
smaller weights than

00:10:13.630 --> 00:10:16.240
would be required
to attack people

00:10:16.240 --> 00:10:19.900
with conventional weapons
or with chemical weapons.

00:10:19.900 --> 00:10:23.080
Simply because of the fact
that a single organism weighing

00:10:23.080 --> 00:10:27.850
about 10 to the minus 12 grams
or a few dozen organisms,

00:10:27.850 --> 00:10:31.750
given the right
infectious organism,

00:10:31.750 --> 00:10:34.690
could initiate an infection.

00:10:34.690 --> 00:10:39.230
So this means that,
in relatively simple--

00:10:39.230 --> 00:10:41.310
not totally simple
and not totally cheap,

00:10:41.310 --> 00:10:44.640
but relatively compared
to other kinds of weapons

00:10:44.640 --> 00:10:48.360
that could have strategic
effects over large areas,

00:10:48.360 --> 00:10:53.640
biological weapons could be a
very serious strategic threat,

00:10:53.640 --> 00:10:56.880
either for intimidation or
for actually causing disease.

00:10:59.580 --> 00:11:01.930
There is possible a
protection, if you're

00:11:01.930 --> 00:11:03.430
wearing a simple air filter.

00:11:03.430 --> 00:11:06.850
But the problem is that for the
attack of large populations,

00:11:06.850 --> 00:11:10.870
unless they're warned,
unless they were equipped,

00:11:10.870 --> 00:11:14.360
they could be very vulnerable.

00:11:14.360 --> 00:11:18.070
So that is a short description
of the kind of hazard

00:11:18.070 --> 00:11:20.720
that's easy to see
today if there were

00:11:20.720 --> 00:11:25.600
a deliberate, intense
exploitation of bacteriology

00:11:25.600 --> 00:11:28.250
for military purposes.

00:11:28.250 --> 00:11:32.830
The future potential
for danger is harder

00:11:32.830 --> 00:11:36.300
and more foggy to describe.

00:11:36.300 --> 00:11:38.710
And, in fact, I'm going to
swing all the way to a very

00:11:38.710 --> 00:11:40.840
distant future and say
something that may sound

00:11:40.840 --> 00:11:42.970
very foggy to some of you.

00:11:42.970 --> 00:11:46.630
And that is that
we've gone through

00:11:46.630 --> 00:11:52.240
many different technological
ages, muscle power, fire--

00:11:52.240 --> 00:11:54.480
the power of fire.

00:11:54.480 --> 00:11:57.780
Then coupled via
various agencies.

00:11:57.780 --> 00:12:01.230
So steam, then an electronic
age, a nuclear age.

00:12:01.230 --> 00:12:05.330
And all of these technologies do
get used for hostile purposes,

00:12:05.330 --> 00:12:08.050
as well as for
constructive purposes.

00:12:08.050 --> 00:12:09.790
Some people might feel--

00:12:09.790 --> 00:12:12.910
and I'm one of them, and I
should admit this up front--

00:12:12.910 --> 00:12:16.430
that those methods
of waging war,

00:12:16.430 --> 00:12:22.720
or more amorphous kinds of
hostile action against other

00:12:22.720 --> 00:12:28.800
members of our species, are
somehow survivable compared

00:12:28.800 --> 00:12:31.590
to what would happen if we used
our knowledge of ourselves,

00:12:31.590 --> 00:12:35.160
if we use the kind of knowledge
of life processes that is

00:12:35.160 --> 00:12:37.920
beginning to accumulate--
not very much of it yet,

00:12:37.920 --> 00:12:40.470
but more and more rapidly
and will become increasingly

00:12:40.470 --> 00:12:43.990
profound until we understand
all of the life processes,

00:12:43.990 --> 00:12:46.380
including those of
development and of the brain--

00:12:46.380 --> 00:12:49.200
that if this kind of
knowledge is deliberately used

00:12:49.200 --> 00:12:52.579
for hostile purposes on
any significant scale,

00:12:52.579 --> 00:12:54.120
that we could be in
a kind of trouble

00:12:54.120 --> 00:12:57.480
as a species that's very
different from the kinds

00:12:57.480 --> 00:13:01.290
of trouble we could have
gotten into with other kinds

00:13:01.290 --> 00:13:04.445
of scientific violence.

00:13:04.445 --> 00:13:08.880
And not just in overt
war, but in other areas

00:13:08.880 --> 00:13:12.840
in which human hostility
can be manifested.

00:13:12.840 --> 00:13:16.380
Well, so much for the
ball-gazing and the philosophy.

00:13:16.380 --> 00:13:20.190
I'd like now to try to give
you a short historical sketch

00:13:20.190 --> 00:13:22.020
of this subject.

00:13:22.020 --> 00:13:26.520
Before World War I
and even in World War

00:13:26.520 --> 00:13:29.550
I there wasn't much really
going on in the area

00:13:29.550 --> 00:13:30.930
of biological warfare.

00:13:30.930 --> 00:13:34.060
After all, the whole idea
of infectious organisms

00:13:34.060 --> 00:13:39.030
and the isolation of pure
cultures wasn't much before

00:13:39.030 --> 00:13:43.570
World War I. And although there
were some biological warfare

00:13:43.570 --> 00:13:45.510
attempts, small-scale--

00:13:45.510 --> 00:13:48.690
giving horses glanders,
some things like that--

00:13:48.690 --> 00:13:51.750
it wasn't until World War
II that it really got going.

00:13:51.750 --> 00:13:55.350
But during World War II there
were sizable biological warfare

00:13:55.350 --> 00:14:00.400
programs in Great Britain, the
United States, Germany, Japan,

00:14:00.400 --> 00:14:03.650
Canada, and almost certainly
in the Soviet Union.

00:14:03.650 --> 00:14:06.800
And we will find out about that
as I'll explain later, we hope,

00:14:06.800 --> 00:14:09.617
very soon.

00:14:09.617 --> 00:14:11.450
We know most about the
United States program

00:14:11.450 --> 00:14:13.520
because the United
States has by far--

00:14:13.520 --> 00:14:15.560
more than the Canadians,
more than the British,

00:14:15.560 --> 00:14:18.290
more than the Japanese,
more than Germany--

00:14:18.290 --> 00:14:20.540
been open about what
it was that we were

00:14:20.540 --> 00:14:24.320
doing, up until the year 1969.

00:14:24.320 --> 00:14:27.290
Lots of different organisms
were screened to see

00:14:27.290 --> 00:14:30.740
which would be most effective
for aerosol dissemination.

00:14:30.740 --> 00:14:35.390
Aerosol dissemination was the
main mode of dissemination

00:14:35.390 --> 00:14:36.350
that was investigated.

00:14:36.350 --> 00:14:37.850
It was early on
realized that it was

00:14:37.850 --> 00:14:41.700
by far the most
effective if one wanted

00:14:41.700 --> 00:14:46.050
effectiveness out of biological
weapons and strategic weapons.

00:14:46.050 --> 00:14:47.790
By the time that
President Nixon ordered

00:14:47.790 --> 00:14:51.270
our stockpiles
destroyed, we had gone

00:14:51.270 --> 00:14:53.730
through several different
infectious organisms,

00:14:53.730 --> 00:14:57.690
standardize them, and then
declared them obsolete.

00:14:57.690 --> 00:15:00.240
But the ones that were
standardized and stockpiled

00:15:00.240 --> 00:15:02.990
at that time were
Francisella tularensis--

00:15:02.990 --> 00:15:05.680
that's the organism
that causes tularemia.

00:15:05.680 --> 00:15:09.030
Maybe four or five cells,
though I don't know how reliable

00:15:09.030 --> 00:15:12.971
these numbers, extrapolated
from quite different situation,

00:15:12.971 --> 00:15:13.470
would be.

00:15:13.470 --> 00:15:16.207
But it was thought that a
small number of organisms

00:15:16.207 --> 00:15:17.040
would be infectious.

00:15:17.040 --> 00:15:18.900
And we had a large stockpile.

00:15:18.900 --> 00:15:22.800
Coxiella burnetti, a
Rickettsia that causes Q fever.

00:15:22.800 --> 00:15:24.690
And Venezuelan
equine encephalitis,

00:15:24.690 --> 00:15:29.920
a virus that was categorized as
an incapacitating rather than

00:15:29.920 --> 00:15:32.010
a lethal biological weapon.

00:15:32.010 --> 00:15:34.200
So we had stockpiles
of these things.

00:15:34.200 --> 00:15:36.420
And there were also
stockpiles of wheat rust

00:15:36.420 --> 00:15:42.540
and rice blast two
anti-crop fungal agents.

00:15:42.540 --> 00:15:44.220
The scale of this
effort was pretty big.

00:15:44.220 --> 00:15:47.010
For example, at Pine Bluff
there were separate facilities

00:15:47.010 --> 00:15:49.410
for each infectious organism.

00:15:49.410 --> 00:15:54.750
In the late '50s one of the
facilities there for growing

00:15:54.750 --> 00:15:55.830
Rickettsia--

00:15:55.830 --> 00:15:58.730
it was called the X201
Facility-- it was fairly big.

00:15:58.730 --> 00:16:01.610
It employed more
than 800 people,

00:16:01.610 --> 00:16:03.060
used two million
gallons of water

00:16:03.060 --> 00:16:07.050
a day, five megawatts of
electricity, lots of steam.

00:16:07.050 --> 00:16:09.430
It was big.

00:16:09.430 --> 00:16:13.310
Partly because, at
least at that time,

00:16:13.310 --> 00:16:16.180
it was not possible,
reliably, to store

00:16:16.180 --> 00:16:17.740
these agents for
very long periods

00:16:17.740 --> 00:16:18.930
and keep them infectious.

00:16:18.930 --> 00:16:21.520
And so the idea was to have
a pretty big capacity that

00:16:21.520 --> 00:16:24.340
could be put online quickly.

00:16:24.340 --> 00:16:26.590
Generally speaking,
protection wasn't

00:16:26.590 --> 00:16:28.580
available to
civilian populations.

00:16:28.580 --> 00:16:29.230
It still isn't.

00:16:29.230 --> 00:16:31.030
It would be a big
chore, as I indicated.

00:16:31.030 --> 00:16:33.070
Although conceivably
it could be done,

00:16:33.070 --> 00:16:36.940
if there was a sense of
acute danger maintained,

00:16:36.940 --> 00:16:42.080
detectors, diagnosis, gas masks,
alarms, discipline, and so on.

00:16:42.080 --> 00:16:44.260
But the capability of the
United States at that time

00:16:44.260 --> 00:16:45.490
was offensive.

00:16:45.490 --> 00:16:49.960
But, as it is today, hardly
any defensive capability.

00:16:49.960 --> 00:16:53.380
At least in the narrow
sense of the word.

00:16:53.380 --> 00:16:54.970
I should now digress
slightly and say

00:16:54.970 --> 00:16:56.178
how I got interested in this.

00:16:56.178 --> 00:16:58.510
It was by total accident.

00:16:58.510 --> 00:17:02.020
During the summer of 1963,
as I only recently learned,

00:17:02.020 --> 00:17:04.780
there was a budget surplus at
the United States Arms Control

00:17:04.780 --> 00:17:08.319
and Disarmament
Agency, and there

00:17:08.319 --> 00:17:12.490
was the alternative of giving it
back or figuring out something

00:17:12.490 --> 00:17:13.569
to do with it.

00:17:13.569 --> 00:17:16.810
And so the science and
technology branch director

00:17:16.810 --> 00:17:20.170
of the time, an excellent
chemist from Montana,

00:17:20.170 --> 00:17:21.130
decided to use it.

00:17:21.130 --> 00:17:24.099
And he invited a
group of academics.

00:17:24.099 --> 00:17:26.230
I shared an office
with Freeman Dyson

00:17:26.230 --> 00:17:29.860
and we were told, do
whatever you want.

00:17:29.860 --> 00:17:32.758
Just do something.

00:17:32.758 --> 00:17:33.750
[AUDIENCE CHUCKLES]

00:17:33.750 --> 00:17:35.429
And I said, well, that's not--

00:17:35.429 --> 00:17:35.970
I don't know.

00:17:35.970 --> 00:17:36.820
What should I do?

00:17:36.820 --> 00:17:38.560
I went down there
just because I thought

00:17:38.560 --> 00:17:40.570
it would be interesting
to see a completely

00:17:40.570 --> 00:17:44.290
different kind of human
activity from molecular biology.

00:17:44.290 --> 00:17:45.700
And just for a summer.

00:17:45.700 --> 00:17:48.760
I didn't think it would risk
losing more than a summer.

00:17:48.760 --> 00:17:51.190
And so I was told, well, why
don't you look into theater

00:17:51.190 --> 00:17:52.930
nuclear arms control in Europe?

00:17:52.930 --> 00:17:55.150
And I tried that for
a couple of weeks.

00:17:55.150 --> 00:17:56.110
[LAUGHTER]

00:17:56.110 --> 00:17:58.660
Then it dawned on me slowly--

00:17:58.660 --> 00:18:00.340
too slowly-- that
there were already

00:18:00.340 --> 00:18:01.650
lots of people in the field.

00:18:01.650 --> 00:18:03.480
And I never liked
to be in a field

00:18:03.480 --> 00:18:05.170
where there are too
many other people.

00:18:05.170 --> 00:18:07.750
And they had written wisely
about the subject and unwisely

00:18:07.750 --> 00:18:11.130
about the subject, and
there was a lot known.

00:18:11.130 --> 00:18:12.790
In two or three months
there was no way

00:18:12.790 --> 00:18:15.430
that I could do anything
useful for the United States

00:18:15.430 --> 00:18:16.304
government.

00:18:16.304 --> 00:18:17.970
So I went to my boss
and said, well, I'm

00:18:17.970 --> 00:18:19.300
a chemist and a biologist.

00:18:19.300 --> 00:18:22.947
Haven't you got something for me
that would draw on what I know?

00:18:22.947 --> 00:18:23.530
He said, yeah.

00:18:23.530 --> 00:18:24.730
We had a guy who did that.

00:18:24.730 --> 00:18:26.440
He was from Caltech, too.

00:18:26.440 --> 00:18:28.090
He got depressed and
he killed himself,

00:18:28.090 --> 00:18:29.697
but you could have his desk.

00:18:29.697 --> 00:18:31.445
[LAUGHTER]

00:18:32.320 --> 00:18:33.590
So I did.

00:18:33.590 --> 00:18:36.440
And, that is, I took his desk.

00:18:36.440 --> 00:18:39.370
[LAUGHTER]

00:18:39.370 --> 00:18:41.770
At first I thought, well,
I'll deal with this subject.

00:18:41.770 --> 00:18:44.800
And then I realized that even
that was too big a subject

00:18:44.800 --> 00:18:46.150
for just a summer.

00:18:46.150 --> 00:18:48.730
So I put the larger
of the two aside--

00:18:48.730 --> 00:18:50.410
that was chemical weapons--

00:18:50.410 --> 00:18:52.240
and concentrated on
biological weapons.

00:18:52.240 --> 00:18:54.850
So I went to Fort Detrick
to see what we were doing.

00:18:54.850 --> 00:18:57.070
This was 1963.

00:18:57.070 --> 00:18:58.630
And I went to the
CIA to see what

00:18:58.630 --> 00:19:00.490
we thought other
people were doing,

00:19:00.490 --> 00:19:02.390
and I talked to lots of people.

00:19:02.390 --> 00:19:04.660
And I came to a rather
simple conclusion.

00:19:04.660 --> 00:19:07.140
Probably too simple,
but it was this.

00:19:07.140 --> 00:19:09.970
It sort of went like a
series of propositions.

00:19:09.970 --> 00:19:12.880
What can you do with
biological weapons?

00:19:12.880 --> 00:19:14.860
You can kill people and
threaten to kill them

00:19:14.860 --> 00:19:18.400
over very big areas, hundreds
of square kilometers.

00:19:18.400 --> 00:19:21.320
The United States
can already do that.

00:19:21.320 --> 00:19:24.590
The way that we can do, or could
have done it, can still do it,

00:19:24.590 --> 00:19:28.820
was very expensive and very
technologically sophisticated,

00:19:28.820 --> 00:19:31.900
namely nuclear weapons.

00:19:31.900 --> 00:19:35.470
Why should we be the ones to
introduce a cheap way of doing

00:19:35.470 --> 00:19:36.230
the same thing?

00:19:36.230 --> 00:19:38.750
What capability
would it add for us?

00:19:38.750 --> 00:19:40.360
Other people, yes.

00:19:40.360 --> 00:19:42.460
They would have a
new capability if we

00:19:42.460 --> 00:19:46.210
were to pioneer the development
of biological weapons.

00:19:46.210 --> 00:19:47.920
Besides, usually what
the United States

00:19:47.920 --> 00:19:52.240
does gets to be known around
the world with a small time lag,

00:19:52.240 --> 00:19:54.820
so the possibility of
doing it all in secret--

00:19:54.820 --> 00:19:57.820
in fact, there were
chemical warfare officers

00:19:57.820 --> 00:20:02.080
that were writing books praising
it because it would save money.

00:20:02.080 --> 00:20:04.690
And I concluded that the
cheaper it was the worse it was,

00:20:04.690 --> 00:20:07.720
and we should definitely stop
it completely and instead

00:20:07.720 --> 00:20:12.130
just concentrate on watching
as carefully as we could what

00:20:12.130 --> 00:20:16.810
other people were doing,
discouraging it, and thinking

00:20:16.810 --> 00:20:19.300
about what to do if it
should ever break loose.

00:20:19.300 --> 00:20:22.840
But nobody at that
time felt that there

00:20:22.840 --> 00:20:24.620
was enough time on
their schedule, no one

00:20:24.620 --> 00:20:28.240
high up enough, to
do much about it.

00:20:28.240 --> 00:20:34.260
When President Nixon was
elected various things

00:20:34.260 --> 00:20:40.290
conspired to cause a review to
be ordered by the president.

00:20:40.290 --> 00:20:42.019
And this was a very
interesting review.

00:20:42.019 --> 00:20:43.560
There are different
kinds of reviews.

00:20:43.560 --> 00:20:45.180
I suppose sometimes
you review something

00:20:45.180 --> 00:20:47.138
because you don't want
to do anything about it,

00:20:47.138 --> 00:20:47.982
so you review it.

00:20:47.982 --> 00:20:50.190
Sometimes you review something
because you've already

00:20:50.190 --> 00:20:52.404
decided what you
want to do about it,

00:20:52.404 --> 00:20:53.820
but you review it
so that it looks

00:20:53.820 --> 00:20:56.520
like you're thinking about
all the different arguments

00:20:56.520 --> 00:20:58.080
and trying to get all the facts.

00:20:58.080 --> 00:21:00.539
And then sometimes you
would review something

00:21:00.539 --> 00:21:02.580
when you really thought
you ought to look into it

00:21:02.580 --> 00:21:05.822
and didn't know what it all
meant and what you should do.

00:21:05.822 --> 00:21:07.530
I could be wrong, but
I think this review

00:21:07.530 --> 00:21:09.990
was of the third kind.

00:21:09.990 --> 00:21:13.290
And it was done in
a style that may

00:21:13.290 --> 00:21:15.490
have been, at least in
some of those reviews,

00:21:15.490 --> 00:21:18.090
characteristic of Henry
Kissinger, which I thought

00:21:18.090 --> 00:21:19.950
was a very admirable style.

00:21:19.950 --> 00:21:22.410
And that was, each
agency of the government

00:21:22.410 --> 00:21:27.570
was not only asked but there was
insistence that it explain not

00:21:27.570 --> 00:21:30.240
only what it thought
was its best policy

00:21:30.240 --> 00:21:33.390
but give the pros and cons
for all the other policies.

00:21:33.390 --> 00:21:36.810
So that this enabled
relatively junior staff people

00:21:36.810 --> 00:21:38.640
from the National
Security Council

00:21:38.640 --> 00:21:42.030
to go over to the Joint
Chiefs, for example, or to ACDA

00:21:42.030 --> 00:21:43.980
or to state and say,
well gentlemen, you've

00:21:43.980 --> 00:21:47.670
done an excellent job here
of explaining your preferred

00:21:47.670 --> 00:21:48.180
option.

00:21:48.180 --> 00:21:51.630
Now, when it comes down
to this option number two,

00:21:51.630 --> 00:21:53.890
you haven't given a very
good argument for it.

00:21:53.890 --> 00:21:55.557
Of course, that wasn't
what they wanted.

00:21:55.557 --> 00:21:57.765
So naturally they didn't
give a good argument for it.

00:21:57.765 --> 00:21:59.730
But because of this
ground rule that you

00:21:59.730 --> 00:22:01.650
had to give the
president every option

00:22:01.650 --> 00:22:05.409
and you had to argue every
option, pros and cons--

00:22:05.409 --> 00:22:07.950
which, so far as I know, maybe
didn't happen in many reviews.

00:22:07.950 --> 00:22:10.050
This is the only
review at that level

00:22:10.050 --> 00:22:12.000
that I've had much to do with.

00:22:12.000 --> 00:22:14.940
But I thought it was
pretty excellent.

00:22:14.940 --> 00:22:16.920
And I do know that what
President Nixon got

00:22:16.920 --> 00:22:18.870
had all the options
and all the arguments

00:22:18.870 --> 00:22:22.370
that at least I could
ever imagine, pro and con.

00:22:22.370 --> 00:22:25.470
And he decided something
more sweeping than I

00:22:25.470 --> 00:22:27.210
think most of us expected.

00:22:27.210 --> 00:22:30.420
He decided to get rid of the
biological warfare program

00:22:30.420 --> 00:22:31.510
completely.

00:22:31.510 --> 00:22:33.300
He was offered many options.

00:22:33.300 --> 00:22:35.730
He was told, you
could renounce it.

00:22:35.730 --> 00:22:37.410
If someone else,
namely the Soviets,

00:22:37.410 --> 00:22:40.920
are willing to do that there
would be a quid pro quo.

00:22:40.920 --> 00:22:43.920
Or you could renounce
it, but mothball it.

00:22:43.920 --> 00:22:46.230
Don't renounce the option,
just get rid of the program

00:22:46.230 --> 00:22:47.010
or tune it down.

00:22:47.010 --> 00:22:49.950
It was running at a pretty
fast clip in those days.

00:22:49.950 --> 00:22:52.600
But keep the option.

00:22:52.600 --> 00:22:54.970
He rejected it
completely and ordered

00:22:54.970 --> 00:22:57.730
that the program be stopped, and
that the United States renounce

00:22:57.730 --> 00:23:01.660
its right ever to use
biological weapons.

00:23:01.660 --> 00:23:04.150
And then, during a
backgrounder some journalists

00:23:04.150 --> 00:23:07.470
asked Henry Kissinger,
well what about toxins?

00:23:07.470 --> 00:23:09.610
And there's a
verbatim text of this.

00:23:09.610 --> 00:23:12.540
And Kissinger says,
what are toxins?

00:23:12.540 --> 00:23:13.480
[CHUCKLES]

00:23:13.480 --> 00:23:15.400
And the journalist
didn't know either.

00:23:15.400 --> 00:23:17.085
Someone had fed
him the question.

00:23:17.085 --> 00:23:19.310
[LAUGHTER]

00:23:19.310 --> 00:23:22.710
It turned out the NSC staff
had written a lot about toxins,

00:23:22.710 --> 00:23:26.520
but the head of that particular
review saw how long it was

00:23:26.520 --> 00:23:29.700
and how technical it looked and
said, take this, reduce this.

00:23:29.700 --> 00:23:32.010
And so what went to the
president about toxins

00:23:32.010 --> 00:23:34.910
was really very brief,
and it got left out.

00:23:34.910 --> 00:23:38.161
And so a whole new review
was ordered up about toxins.

00:23:38.161 --> 00:23:39.660
And again, the
president was offered

00:23:39.660 --> 00:23:42.930
a whole range of options
from, toxins are not living.

00:23:42.930 --> 00:23:43.890
They do not reproduce.

00:23:43.890 --> 00:23:44.390
You know?

00:23:44.390 --> 00:23:48.076
Toxins are defined as poisonous
things, poisonous chemicals--

00:23:48.076 --> 00:23:48.950
they don't reproduce.

00:23:48.950 --> 00:23:52.380
They're chemicals-- but that
are made by living things.

00:23:52.380 --> 00:23:55.050
So the clostridium
botulinum makes

00:23:55.050 --> 00:23:57.840
a nasty toxin called botulin.

00:23:57.840 --> 00:24:02.110
Yeast makes a pleasurable
toxin called ethanol.

00:24:02.110 --> 00:24:07.050
It's still a toxin in the sense
that it, in appropriate dose

00:24:07.050 --> 00:24:07.770
can be harmful.

00:24:07.770 --> 00:24:10.760
And it's made by a living thing.

00:24:10.760 --> 00:24:14.730
Anyway, one option was,
toxins are not infectious.

00:24:14.730 --> 00:24:16.020
They're not BW.

00:24:16.020 --> 00:24:17.640
We're not going
to renounce them.

00:24:17.640 --> 00:24:20.760
Another was, toxins, if
made by living things--

00:24:20.760 --> 00:24:23.040
because bacteria make
toxins and that's

00:24:23.040 --> 00:24:25.800
getting close to sounding
like disease again--

00:24:25.800 --> 00:24:27.660
would be prohibited
and renounced.

00:24:27.660 --> 00:24:29.190
But if the chemists
over there can

00:24:29.190 --> 00:24:31.610
learn how to synthesize
them that would be OK.

00:24:31.610 --> 00:24:32.610
[LAUGHTER]

00:24:32.610 --> 00:24:34.560
Another option was,
no, we'll renounce them

00:24:34.560 --> 00:24:38.370
under all circumstances, but
we won't renounce the right

00:24:38.370 --> 00:24:41.040
to use them in future
if other people do.

00:24:41.040 --> 00:24:44.430
And then finally there's an
extreme, exceedingly left-wing

00:24:44.430 --> 00:24:45.730
alternative.

00:24:45.730 --> 00:24:48.390
Renounce them, no matter how
they're made, and renounced

00:24:48.390 --> 00:24:49.470
them in perpetuity.

00:24:49.470 --> 00:24:51.930
And that's what
President Nixon did.

00:24:51.930 --> 00:24:56.040
The first decision about
infectious agents in 1969,

00:24:56.040 --> 00:24:59.330
November, and the second one
about toxins after someone

00:24:59.330 --> 00:25:02.830
asked, what are toxins, in 1970.

00:25:02.830 --> 00:25:05.820
And this really got the United
States out of the business.

00:25:05.820 --> 00:25:08.610
We destroyed our stockpiles.

00:25:08.610 --> 00:25:11.970
We converted the
laboratories at Pine Bluff

00:25:11.970 --> 00:25:16.050
to the study of toxic substances
in the laboratories at Fort

00:25:16.050 --> 00:25:16.650
Detrick--

00:25:16.650 --> 00:25:19.830
and the production facilities--
and the laboratories at Fort

00:25:19.830 --> 00:25:23.700
Detrick to cancer
research and other things.

00:25:23.700 --> 00:25:28.860
There were kept at those
sites military units that

00:25:28.860 --> 00:25:31.980
did other things, and
a biological defensive

00:25:31.980 --> 00:25:36.030
enterprise, but not making
weapons at Fort Detrick.

00:25:36.030 --> 00:25:39.670
And also a biological
defensively-oriented facility

00:25:39.670 --> 00:25:41.010
at Dugway in Utah.

00:25:41.010 --> 00:25:44.370
But the offensive program
was really extirpated.

00:25:44.370 --> 00:25:46.140
Gone.

00:25:46.140 --> 00:25:49.150
Then there came up the question,
well, how about a treaty?

00:25:49.150 --> 00:25:51.090
Well, we had
already given it up.

00:25:51.090 --> 00:25:52.980
And not only that, we'd
renounced the option.

00:25:52.980 --> 00:25:57.240
So a treaty then
would be only a bonus.

00:25:57.240 --> 00:26:02.040
If people didn't obey it,
that wouldn't be so good.

00:26:02.040 --> 00:26:03.960
But since it didn't
require us to do anything

00:26:03.960 --> 00:26:06.210
we weren't going to
do anyway, why not?

00:26:06.210 --> 00:26:08.389
And the British had
proposed such a treaty,

00:26:08.389 --> 00:26:09.930
and so at the same
time the president

00:26:09.930 --> 00:26:13.200
renounced BW he announced
support for that British draft

00:26:13.200 --> 00:26:16.140
treaty which is called
the Biological Weapons

00:26:16.140 --> 00:26:18.360
Convention of 1972.

00:26:18.360 --> 00:26:20.850
Today there are about
100 parties to it.

00:26:20.850 --> 00:26:27.540
It prohibits the production--
it prohibits the development,

00:26:27.540 --> 00:26:28.940
production,
possession, transfer,

00:26:28.940 --> 00:26:34.070
et cetera of biological agents
for any but peaceful purposes.

00:26:34.070 --> 00:26:36.800
It didn't have any
verification attached to it,

00:26:36.800 --> 00:26:42.920
but it did have an article
which required a review

00:26:42.920 --> 00:26:44.990
conference after five years.

00:26:44.990 --> 00:26:48.050
And it's become customary
to have review conferences

00:26:48.050 --> 00:26:48.720
periodically.

00:26:48.720 --> 00:26:50.300
There have now been three.

00:26:50.300 --> 00:26:52.850
And these review
conferences have

00:26:52.850 --> 00:26:56.150
achieved what some would call
a little bit of verification,

00:26:56.150 --> 00:26:59.360
and what others would call
some confidence-building.

00:26:59.360 --> 00:27:01.190
The first review
conference in 1980

00:27:01.190 --> 00:27:02.390
didn't accomplish very much.

00:27:02.390 --> 00:27:05.810
This was, at the time,
shortly after in December

00:27:05.810 --> 00:27:07.880
that the Soviets had
invaded Afghanistan.

00:27:07.880 --> 00:27:09.590
The Cold War was very bitter.

00:27:09.590 --> 00:27:12.530
[INAUDIBLE] There had been an
outbreak of anthrax and had

00:27:12.530 --> 00:27:15.800
become known in the
West in April of 1979--

00:27:15.800 --> 00:27:19.760
became known in the
West shortly after that.

00:27:19.760 --> 00:27:22.990
And these subjects dominated
much of the debates

00:27:22.990 --> 00:27:24.440
at that first review conference.

00:27:24.440 --> 00:27:28.250
But the second one in 1986
did achieve something.

00:27:28.250 --> 00:27:30.350
The parties to the
Biological Weapons Convention

00:27:30.350 --> 00:27:34.130
agreed that they would declare
the location, ownership, size,

00:27:34.130 --> 00:27:38.210
and general purpose
of all P4 facilities.

00:27:38.210 --> 00:27:41.280
P4, as you know, is a
high level of containment

00:27:41.280 --> 00:27:45.480
for exceedingly-dangerous
infectious agents.

00:27:45.480 --> 00:27:48.360
And that they would also
declare the location, et cetera

00:27:48.360 --> 00:27:52.410
of all P3 facilities
if they were engaged

00:27:52.410 --> 00:27:55.410
in work of direct relevance
to the Biological Weapons

00:27:55.410 --> 00:27:56.400
Convention.

00:27:56.400 --> 00:27:58.710
And pursuant to that
these declarations,

00:27:58.710 --> 00:28:01.350
which the United States and the
Soviet Union and Great Britain

00:28:01.350 --> 00:28:03.630
and many countries--
but also many didn't--

00:28:03.630 --> 00:28:06.035
but many countries--
every April the 15th they

00:28:06.035 --> 00:28:08.550
are supposed to renew
their declarations.

00:28:08.550 --> 00:28:10.770
And this has been going
on ever since, the United

00:28:10.770 --> 00:28:15.760
States and the Soviet Union have
been filing these declarations.

00:28:15.760 --> 00:28:19.490
The third review conference
was last year in 1991,

00:28:19.490 --> 00:28:21.940
and it added some
more measures--

00:28:21.940 --> 00:28:24.650
states parties to the
convention which, as I say,

00:28:24.650 --> 00:28:27.310
has about 120 parties today--

00:28:27.310 --> 00:28:30.880
agreed that they would describe
their biological defense

00:28:30.880 --> 00:28:31.381
programs.

00:28:31.381 --> 00:28:33.755
They're not supposed to have
biological offense programs,

00:28:33.755 --> 00:28:36.010
so there'd be no point asking
them to describe those--

00:28:36.010 --> 00:28:38.080
but their biological
defense programs.

00:28:38.080 --> 00:28:41.110
Namely, how they're
organized, actually

00:28:41.110 --> 00:28:43.690
giving names of people in
charge of the major components,

00:28:43.690 --> 00:28:45.437
what their facilities
are, and so on.

00:28:45.437 --> 00:28:46.520
Now, you could dodge this.

00:28:46.520 --> 00:28:49.180
You could say, it's actually
my cotton candy program.

00:28:49.180 --> 00:28:51.200
It's not a biological
defense program at all.

00:28:51.200 --> 00:28:52.240
It's something else.

00:28:52.240 --> 00:28:56.170
But it allows one to
compare both what you know

00:28:56.170 --> 00:28:58.750
and what you fear
with what is declared.

00:28:58.750 --> 00:29:01.870
And by comparison
of this information,

00:29:01.870 --> 00:29:05.410
it's possible to derive
conclusions about compliance

00:29:05.410 --> 00:29:06.725
or about noncompliance.

00:29:06.725 --> 00:29:08.350
They may not be rock
solid, but they're

00:29:08.350 --> 00:29:13.090
better than the conclusions
that you could otherwise make.

00:29:13.090 --> 00:29:17.110
A very interesting agreement
was that all states parties

00:29:17.110 --> 00:29:21.700
agreed to submit by April
15 of this year, income tax

00:29:21.700 --> 00:29:25.180
day, a description of their
biological warfare activities

00:29:25.180 --> 00:29:29.520
beginning in the year 1946.

00:29:29.520 --> 00:29:31.220
So we're waiting very eagerly.

00:29:31.220 --> 00:29:33.747
We know what the United States
was doing since 1946 pretty

00:29:33.747 --> 00:29:35.330
much, but there are
a lot of countries

00:29:35.330 --> 00:29:37.040
which never said anything.

00:29:37.040 --> 00:29:38.450
The Soviet Union has said--

00:29:38.450 --> 00:29:42.050
when there was a Soviet Union--
that they were in compliance,

00:29:42.050 --> 00:29:44.600
But it will be very
interesting to find out

00:29:44.600 --> 00:29:48.150
what they say they were
doing beginning in 1946.

00:29:48.150 --> 00:29:49.900
And there are some
other governments, too.

00:29:49.900 --> 00:29:53.510
It would be interesting
to know what they say.

00:29:53.510 --> 00:29:56.480
And it was agreed to have a
further meeting very soon.

00:29:56.480 --> 00:29:58.520
Not with the usual
five-year wait--

00:29:58.520 --> 00:30:00.080
in fact, this March--

00:30:00.080 --> 00:30:05.150
of experts to discuss possible
methods of verification.

00:30:05.150 --> 00:30:06.410
There is a disagreement here.

00:30:06.410 --> 00:30:08.540
The United States
generally has argued--

00:30:08.540 --> 00:30:11.600
to some extent it's a hangover
from the etiological days

00:30:11.600 --> 00:30:14.690
of the Reagan administration,
but to some extent

00:30:14.690 --> 00:30:16.886
there's a lot of
merit to it, that--

00:30:16.886 --> 00:30:18.260
and it doesn't
matter whether you

00:30:18.260 --> 00:30:22.130
say a ban on biological
weapons is unverifiable

00:30:22.130 --> 00:30:24.200
or it's not verifiable enough.

00:30:24.200 --> 00:30:26.590
Its theology to try to
make that distinction.

00:30:26.590 --> 00:30:29.660
But the United States has argued
that it's not verifiable enough

00:30:29.660 --> 00:30:33.270
to have a verification regime.

00:30:33.270 --> 00:30:35.660
So we could call them
confidence-building measures

00:30:35.660 --> 00:30:36.340
instead.

00:30:36.340 --> 00:30:39.650
I think at some points
it ends up as a quibble.

00:30:39.650 --> 00:30:42.020
Except for a concept
that is pretty obvious,

00:30:42.020 --> 00:30:44.880
but I would like to take a
minute and talk about it.

00:30:44.880 --> 00:30:49.240
And that is, yes, it could
be difficult to verify

00:30:49.240 --> 00:30:51.580
a biological disarmament
treaty, although there

00:30:51.580 --> 00:30:54.130
are things you can look for
if you know where to look,

00:30:54.130 --> 00:30:57.160
certain kinds of storage
facilities, aero-biology,

00:30:57.160 --> 00:30:58.870
aerosolization chambers.

00:30:58.870 --> 00:31:01.270
What kind, what size,
what precautions.

00:31:01.270 --> 00:31:03.037
Studies of
non-endemic pathogens.

00:31:03.037 --> 00:31:04.120
Why are you studying that?

00:31:04.120 --> 00:31:05.860
You don't have that
in your country.

00:31:05.860 --> 00:31:07.490
If the explanation
is reasonable, fine.

00:31:07.490 --> 00:31:09.310
If not, keep asking questions.

00:31:09.310 --> 00:31:11.410
Very large-scale production
units, though those

00:31:11.410 --> 00:31:13.240
may exist for other
purposes, too.

00:31:13.240 --> 00:31:15.310
Trace the organization
in financing.

00:31:15.310 --> 00:31:18.680
Look for test grounds,
and so on and so forth.

00:31:18.680 --> 00:31:20.770
But there's another way--

00:31:20.770 --> 00:31:22.480
not to exclude those
others-- but there's

00:31:22.480 --> 00:31:24.521
another way of dealing
with this kind of problem.

00:31:24.521 --> 00:31:27.040
And that is based
on the assumption

00:31:27.040 --> 00:31:30.520
that nobody is going to develop
biological weapons in the open.

00:31:30.520 --> 00:31:34.360
You're not supposed to do it,
so you shouldn't do it publicly.

00:31:34.360 --> 00:31:37.930
Therefore, that would mean that
if you could verify openness

00:31:37.930 --> 00:31:40.690
then you would be
verifying compliance.

00:31:40.690 --> 00:31:43.240
Maybe openness could be
viewed as a real object,

00:31:43.240 --> 00:31:46.210
like a cube of platinum.

00:31:46.210 --> 00:31:47.657
Can't you verify openness?

00:31:47.657 --> 00:31:49.490
Yes, there are ways you
can verify openness.

00:31:49.490 --> 00:31:53.230
For example, much better
than checking on the volume

00:31:53.230 --> 00:31:55.760
of their aerosol chambers.

00:31:55.760 --> 00:31:59.230
If my daughter was
the biological safety

00:31:59.230 --> 00:32:04.330
officer at a suspect facility,
considering how snoopy--

00:32:04.330 --> 00:32:06.040
I hope I'm not
insulting anybody--

00:32:06.040 --> 00:32:06.790
[LAUGHTER]

00:32:06.790 --> 00:32:08.520
--biological safety
officers are.

00:32:08.520 --> 00:32:10.930
They go into everything.

00:32:10.930 --> 00:32:12.910
I'd feel very happy
about that facility.

00:32:12.910 --> 00:32:15.160
I'd say there's nothing
that-- there can't be anything

00:32:15.160 --> 00:32:17.176
wrong there if she's there.

00:32:17.176 --> 00:32:18.550
And she talks to
me on the phone.

00:32:18.550 --> 00:32:20.560
I can tell her voice,
it's not funny.

00:32:20.560 --> 00:32:22.090
She comes home for visits.

00:32:22.090 --> 00:32:23.560
I go there and see her.

00:32:23.560 --> 00:32:26.610
What I'm getting at is that,
the exchange of personnel

00:32:26.610 --> 00:32:30.220
is an awfully good way
to verify openness.

00:32:30.220 --> 00:32:33.350
And especially certain
kinds of personnel.

00:32:33.350 --> 00:32:34.560
And there are other ways.

00:32:34.560 --> 00:32:37.420
And probably some ingenious ways
that haven't been thought of.

00:32:37.420 --> 00:32:40.540
But of verifying
openness as an entity

00:32:40.540 --> 00:32:43.160
so that you don't have to get
down to the little details of,

00:32:43.160 --> 00:32:44.660
well, they haven't
got one of those.

00:32:44.660 --> 00:32:47.020
And then someone says, but
maybe you have one of these.

00:32:47.020 --> 00:32:49.520
And they could do the job with
one of those instead of that,

00:32:49.520 --> 00:32:51.020
and so on.

00:32:51.020 --> 00:32:51.670
OK.

00:32:51.670 --> 00:32:55.000
So although this treaty is
not one which has all kinds

00:32:55.000 --> 00:32:57.310
of rigorous verification
attached to it,

00:32:57.310 --> 00:32:59.440
it is one to which
have been added--

00:32:59.440 --> 00:33:02.140
and now with a changed world
environment we hope there will

00:33:02.140 --> 00:33:03.430
be added more and more--

00:33:03.430 --> 00:33:06.190
measures of openness and
of confidence-building.

00:33:06.190 --> 00:33:09.820
And since we don't want anything
to do with biological weapons--

00:33:09.820 --> 00:33:11.650
I hope that none
of our officials

00:33:11.650 --> 00:33:13.690
want anything to do
with biological weapons,

00:33:13.690 --> 00:33:16.510
except for a few souls
at relatively low levels.

00:33:16.510 --> 00:33:20.950
I've never met anybody who
seems to want to anymore.

00:33:20.950 --> 00:33:23.412
We're out of it anyway.

00:33:23.412 --> 00:33:25.370
Maybe there are some
things we should be doing.

00:33:25.370 --> 00:33:28.600
We should be interested in
methods of rapid diagnosis.

00:33:28.600 --> 00:33:31.090
We should be interested
in how to make vaccines

00:33:31.090 --> 00:33:33.190
and how to make some
of them quickly.

00:33:33.190 --> 00:33:35.650
We should have a good
epidemiological surveillance

00:33:35.650 --> 00:33:36.150
service.

00:33:36.150 --> 00:33:39.710
I think ours in recent
years has slipped a bit.

00:33:39.710 --> 00:33:41.540
We should be doing those things.

00:33:41.540 --> 00:33:44.210
There are some things that
are arguable that we do.

00:33:44.210 --> 00:33:45.800
I won't get into those.

00:33:45.800 --> 00:33:49.179
There's room to
argue on both sides.

00:33:49.179 --> 00:33:50.720
But what I would
assert very strongly

00:33:50.720 --> 00:33:52.220
is, we shouldn't do
anything that isn't open.

00:33:52.220 --> 00:33:53.340
It should be open.

00:33:53.340 --> 00:33:55.310
There are some slight
risks in openness,

00:33:55.310 --> 00:33:58.460
if you're working in defense
and diagnosis and detection.

00:33:58.460 --> 00:34:01.100
But I think that so long as
you try and get quid pro quos,

00:34:01.100 --> 00:34:03.920
get other people open, but
be willing to open yourself,

00:34:03.920 --> 00:34:07.260
that you're ahead.

00:34:07.260 --> 00:34:09.310
Now, what things
are there to watch

00:34:09.310 --> 00:34:15.100
coming up in this area
of biological hostility?

00:34:15.100 --> 00:34:17.469
Well, we could start with Iraq.

00:34:17.469 --> 00:34:21.840
We thought that there might be
a hazard emanating from Iraq,

00:34:21.840 --> 00:34:24.719
to the extent that we
vaccinated soldiers

00:34:24.719 --> 00:34:29.250
against bacillus
anthracis and some of them

00:34:29.250 --> 00:34:32.757
against botulinum,
botulisnus toxin.

00:34:32.757 --> 00:34:35.090
I thought I was going to be
on that UN team for a while,

00:34:35.090 --> 00:34:37.480
so I was vaccinated
with bacillus anthracis.

00:34:37.480 --> 00:34:40.840
There was a concern on the
part of several countries

00:34:40.840 --> 00:34:43.179
that maybe there
was a program there.

00:34:43.179 --> 00:34:44.920
And it turns out,
according to the Iraqis

00:34:44.920 --> 00:34:46.370
now, that there was a program.

00:34:46.370 --> 00:34:48.710
They say that it was
at the research level

00:34:48.710 --> 00:34:53.889
and nothing has been detected
in the nature of actual weapons

00:34:53.889 --> 00:34:56.260
production or weapons filling.

00:34:56.260 --> 00:34:59.350
They say that they did have
a military program though.

00:34:59.350 --> 00:35:02.980
Their declaration says that they
worked with bacillus anthracis,

00:35:02.980 --> 00:35:06.670
with clostridium botulinum,
and clostridium perfringens.

00:35:06.670 --> 00:35:09.850
The latter two making
toxins, and anthrax

00:35:09.850 --> 00:35:12.880
being an infectious organism
of which many of you, I think,

00:35:12.880 --> 00:35:14.260
know a little bit about it.

00:35:14.260 --> 00:35:17.530
Lethal, infectious--
a lethal disease

00:35:17.530 --> 00:35:21.119
that can be spread by
inhalation or ingestion.

00:35:21.119 --> 00:35:22.660
So they were working
on these things.

00:35:22.660 --> 00:35:27.220
And there was a secured,
refrigerated storage facility

00:35:27.220 --> 00:35:30.040
that you could argue
might have had in mind

00:35:30.040 --> 00:35:32.260
doing something bad with it.

00:35:34.830 --> 00:35:37.720
That's all the UN
has found to date.

00:35:37.720 --> 00:35:40.970
But there was some
work going on.

00:35:40.970 --> 00:35:43.730
In the next few weeks we should
be learning more about what

00:35:43.730 --> 00:35:44.930
went on in the Soviet Union.

00:35:44.930 --> 00:35:48.200
As I say, there is a requirement
for declaration on April 15.

00:35:48.200 --> 00:35:50.450
And you probably read
in the newspapers

00:35:50.450 --> 00:35:53.330
that Boris Yeltsin and
his military advisor

00:35:53.330 --> 00:35:55.370
on his recent trip
to United States

00:35:55.370 --> 00:35:57.650
have spoken to
American officials

00:35:57.650 --> 00:36:00.890
about some things which
they say went beyond what

00:36:00.890 --> 00:36:02.580
the treaty would have allowed.

00:36:02.580 --> 00:36:05.510
And we'll see what those were.

00:36:05.510 --> 00:36:07.580
We might even see--
though, perhaps,

00:36:07.580 --> 00:36:10.100
less political
incentive for it--

00:36:10.100 --> 00:36:15.020
US admissions of error in
some things that we have said.

00:36:15.020 --> 00:36:18.090
Particularly I have in mind the
allegation that we made again

00:36:18.090 --> 00:36:21.000
and again-- and unfortunately
even repeated this last year

00:36:21.000 --> 00:36:23.830
in what's called the
president's Annual Noncompliance

00:36:23.830 --> 00:36:24.900
Statement--

00:36:24.900 --> 00:36:28.860
the allegation that
somebody was practicing

00:36:28.860 --> 00:36:32.520
trichothecene mycotoxin
warfare in Southeast Asia.

00:36:32.520 --> 00:36:34.980
So far as I know that
"yellow rain" story was just

00:36:34.980 --> 00:36:36.690
completely wrong.

00:36:36.690 --> 00:36:37.764
There was no such--

00:36:37.764 --> 00:36:39.930
there is, as far as I know,
no good evidence for it,

00:36:39.930 --> 00:36:44.340
and a lot of evidence of the
presence of poor reasoning

00:36:44.340 --> 00:36:48.980
and poor data, and even misuse
of science in that story.

00:36:48.980 --> 00:36:51.100
There'll be the March
verification conference

00:36:51.100 --> 00:36:55.970
in Geneva, and that might
agree on further measures

00:36:55.970 --> 00:36:57.320
of verification.

00:36:57.320 --> 00:36:59.870
I don't expect
anything major from it,

00:36:59.870 --> 00:37:03.290
but rather something in
the nature of slow progress

00:37:03.290 --> 00:37:07.800
towards further opening
and confidence-building.

00:37:07.800 --> 00:37:10.164
There's something big though
that's on the horizon,

00:37:10.164 --> 00:37:11.955
and that's the Chemical
Weapons Convention.

00:37:11.955 --> 00:37:15.400
And it's related to biological
weapons because of toxins.

00:37:15.400 --> 00:37:19.210
Now, I haven't said much about
toxins yet, so now I should.

00:37:19.210 --> 00:37:24.820
I'm going to choose to do
that in the course of making

00:37:24.820 --> 00:37:26.800
comments about what
some people say

00:37:26.800 --> 00:37:29.460
is the possible
impact of new biology

00:37:29.460 --> 00:37:32.710
and making more effective
biological weapons.

00:37:32.710 --> 00:37:36.730
I've never believed that
recombinant DNA technology

00:37:36.730 --> 00:37:41.770
or new biological technology
would make infectious organisms

00:37:41.770 --> 00:37:45.140
into a much worse threat
than they already are.

00:37:45.140 --> 00:37:47.144
Now, there are two ways
of understanding that.

00:37:47.144 --> 00:37:49.060
Or, there's really one
way of understanding it

00:37:49.060 --> 00:37:52.480
and one way of
misunderstanding it.

00:37:52.480 --> 00:37:54.719
One way of misunderstanding
it is to say,

00:37:54.719 --> 00:37:57.010
these things aren't threatening
with either old biology

00:37:57.010 --> 00:37:57.820
or new biology.

00:37:57.820 --> 00:37:59.530
That's not what I mean.

00:37:59.530 --> 00:38:01.490
What I mean is
that the old ones--

00:38:01.490 --> 00:38:04.580
well, no one ever did it,
but it's a very good chance

00:38:04.580 --> 00:38:07.360
that the old ones were
already bad enough.

00:38:07.360 --> 00:38:10.300
That pasteurella tularensis--
an awful lot of research

00:38:10.300 --> 00:38:10.960
was done on it.

00:38:13.922 --> 00:38:15.630
You'd have to write
down a list of things

00:38:15.630 --> 00:38:17.220
that would make it
more threatening,

00:38:17.220 --> 00:38:18.678
and I would look
at it, and I don't

00:38:18.678 --> 00:38:20.370
think I would find
it more threatening.

00:38:20.370 --> 00:38:21.180
Four organisms?

00:38:21.180 --> 00:38:22.680
Well, so you might
develop something

00:38:22.680 --> 00:38:24.810
where one organism is enough.

00:38:24.810 --> 00:38:26.580
There's no difference.

00:38:26.580 --> 00:38:29.740
It's fairly stable,
et cetera, et cetera.

00:38:29.740 --> 00:38:32.670
So I've never agreed that
recombinant DNA technology

00:38:32.670 --> 00:38:36.120
makes infectious biological
warfare look that much more

00:38:36.120 --> 00:38:39.180
ominous in any practical
sense that anyone has ever

00:38:39.180 --> 00:38:40.810
been able to describe to me.

00:38:40.810 --> 00:38:44.712
Now toxins, to some extent,
are a different matter.

00:38:44.712 --> 00:38:46.170
By the way, on
infectious organisms

00:38:46.170 --> 00:38:49.200
I should say, though, that
recombinant DNA technology,

00:38:49.200 --> 00:38:52.320
or more modern methods of
doing biological research,

00:38:52.320 --> 00:38:54.240
can make better
vaccines and make--

00:38:54.240 --> 00:38:58.770
we haven't got many yet, but
in principle can make vaccines

00:38:58.770 --> 00:39:01.940
better, can make them easier.

00:39:01.940 --> 00:39:05.370
Now, that's on the defensive
side, not the offensive side.

00:39:05.370 --> 00:39:07.620
But toxins are
something else again.

00:39:07.620 --> 00:39:10.830
There are toxins that
are very poisonous.

00:39:10.830 --> 00:39:14.400
Getting them to act
through the skin and--

00:39:14.400 --> 00:39:16.080
after all, there are
chemical weapons,

00:39:16.080 --> 00:39:18.887
nerve agents that are
also very poisonous,

00:39:18.887 --> 00:39:20.220
and they do go through the skin.

00:39:20.220 --> 00:39:22.350
They're very stable
and they don't burn up

00:39:22.350 --> 00:39:24.790
when they are detonated
with an explosive.

00:39:24.790 --> 00:39:28.384
And militarily they have
all kinds of advantages,

00:39:28.384 --> 00:39:29.550
if that's what one is after.

00:39:29.550 --> 00:39:31.569
And toxins don't get
you much beyond that.

00:39:31.569 --> 00:39:32.860
They don't go through the skin.

00:39:32.860 --> 00:39:35.790
But in principle maybe someone
could engineer them or mix them

00:39:35.790 --> 00:39:36.750
or get them to do that.

00:39:36.750 --> 00:39:39.930
And you could go to higher
levels of toxicity with toxins,

00:39:39.930 --> 00:39:40.512
and so on.

00:39:40.512 --> 00:39:42.720
And there's some other things
you can say about them.

00:39:42.720 --> 00:39:45.840
So maybe there is some
room if you really

00:39:45.840 --> 00:39:50.280
want biological weapons to
do something with toxins

00:39:50.280 --> 00:39:53.250
if you felt you wanted them.

00:39:53.250 --> 00:39:55.290
But the Chemical Weapons
Convention, which is--

00:39:55.290 --> 00:39:57.630
now I've finished the few
technical remarks I had about

00:39:57.630 --> 00:39:58.530
toxins--

00:39:58.530 --> 00:40:01.680
covers toxins as well
as other chemicals.

00:40:01.680 --> 00:40:04.740
And it's a very
amazing convention.

00:40:04.740 --> 00:40:06.180
And we're going to get it--

00:40:06.180 --> 00:40:08.280
probably it will be
initialed this year.

00:40:10.864 --> 00:40:12.030
I say we're going to get it.

00:40:12.030 --> 00:40:14.370
I'd be surprised if we don't.

00:40:14.370 --> 00:40:17.210
It looks very much
as though we will.

00:40:17.210 --> 00:40:19.820
This is a treaty which
will involve many nations.

00:40:19.820 --> 00:40:22.580
It's a multilateral treaty.

00:40:22.580 --> 00:40:24.890
It states parties will
agree to declare and then

00:40:24.890 --> 00:40:29.150
destroy their present holdings
of poison gas weapons, chemical

00:40:29.150 --> 00:40:32.480
weapons, to destroy their
facilities for making them,

00:40:32.480 --> 00:40:37.700
and to institute a verification
regime which, far from perfect,

00:40:37.700 --> 00:40:40.850
nevertheless will certainly
raise the likelihood of getting

00:40:40.850 --> 00:40:43.640
caught if you want to
cheat, and will constitute

00:40:43.640 --> 00:40:45.080
some kind of deterrent.

00:40:45.080 --> 00:40:49.790
And over time will reinforce--
or create and reinforce a norm

00:40:49.790 --> 00:40:52.460
against the use
of toxic weapons.

00:40:52.460 --> 00:40:54.260
And will also provide a model--

00:40:54.260 --> 00:40:56.500
or, better yet, a test
or an experiment--

00:40:56.500 --> 00:40:59.390
of whether we really are
anywhere near a new world

00:40:59.390 --> 00:41:02.420
order, something in which
it's possible to act more

00:41:02.420 --> 00:41:06.652
cooperatively than in the
past to achieve security.

00:41:06.652 --> 00:41:09.110
The Chemical Weapons Convention
will do all of those things

00:41:09.110 --> 00:41:11.380
if it succeeds.

00:41:11.380 --> 00:41:12.820
I have one great
concern about it,

00:41:12.820 --> 00:41:15.180
which is a little bit
off my topic tonight,

00:41:15.180 --> 00:41:16.740
but I do want to
pass it on to you

00:41:16.740 --> 00:41:20.570
because I'm concerned about it.

00:41:20.570 --> 00:41:25.120
Nearly all nations
have the view that--

00:41:25.120 --> 00:41:27.880
nearly all nations have
expressed a view on this,

00:41:27.880 --> 00:41:29.770
that the Geneva
Protocol prohibits

00:41:29.770 --> 00:41:34.090
the use of all toxic
chemicals as means of war.

00:41:34.090 --> 00:41:36.670
And that would include
incapacitating chemicals

00:41:36.670 --> 00:41:39.910
as well as lethal ones.

00:41:39.910 --> 00:41:42.970
The United States has
renounced lethal chemicals

00:41:42.970 --> 00:41:45.310
and has renounced so-called
incapacitating chemicals,

00:41:45.310 --> 00:41:48.460
but has not renounced the use
of what are called "riot control

00:41:48.460 --> 00:41:49.255
agents" in warfare.

00:41:51.900 --> 00:41:54.060
There are some reasons
why riot control agents

00:41:54.060 --> 00:41:55.290
can be useful in warfare.

00:41:55.290 --> 00:41:59.070
We used about 8,000
tons of them in Vietnam.

00:41:59.070 --> 00:42:01.700
Never, essentially--
once, I believe--

00:42:01.700 --> 00:42:04.100
for separating
civilians from soldiers.

00:42:04.100 --> 00:42:07.170
In the overwhelming
majority of cases in order

00:42:07.170 --> 00:42:09.270
to enhance mobility
or firepower,

00:42:09.270 --> 00:42:16.140
dropping CS before bombing runs
and using CS in 155 artillery

00:42:16.140 --> 00:42:17.640
projectiles, and so on.

00:42:17.640 --> 00:42:20.640
And it can be, I
think, marginally

00:42:20.640 --> 00:42:22.980
useful to the side that
uses it, especially

00:42:22.980 --> 00:42:25.630
if the other side
doesn't use it.

00:42:25.630 --> 00:42:27.420
But it is a kind of
chemical warfare.

00:42:27.420 --> 00:42:29.400
Soldiers are wearing gas masks.

00:42:29.400 --> 00:42:31.380
Military establishments
are learning

00:42:31.380 --> 00:42:35.160
how to make offensive
use of toxic chemicals

00:42:35.160 --> 00:42:36.570
that go through the air.

00:42:36.570 --> 00:42:39.780
Establishments back
home are becoming

00:42:39.780 --> 00:42:41.770
dependent for
budgets and careers

00:42:41.770 --> 00:42:45.360
and so on, on a role for
toxic things, and so on.

00:42:45.360 --> 00:42:48.540
In this new convention,
in the article

00:42:48.540 --> 00:42:52.030
which defines what chemicals
are to be prohibited,

00:42:52.030 --> 00:42:54.970
there is a bracketed
pair of sentences

00:42:54.970 --> 00:42:58.210
put there by the United
States which would totally

00:42:58.210 --> 00:43:02.110
exempt all chemicals with
the lethality below 10

00:43:02.110 --> 00:43:06.580
milligrams per kilogram body
weight of Sprague-Dawley

00:43:06.580 --> 00:43:11.710
rat from the convention if
they are used by a state party

00:43:11.710 --> 00:43:18.200
also for law enforcement or
police purposes domestically.

00:43:18.200 --> 00:43:20.120
This bothers me.

00:43:20.120 --> 00:43:22.400
There's now research
going on to try and get

00:43:22.400 --> 00:43:25.430
riot control agents that
are better than CS, which,

00:43:25.430 --> 00:43:28.310
incidentally, stands for the
two Harvard chemists who first

00:43:28.310 --> 00:43:31.400
synthesized it,
Corson and Stoughton.

00:43:31.400 --> 00:43:32.979
It doesn't last very long.

00:43:32.979 --> 00:43:34.520
For example, there's
the whole family

00:43:34.520 --> 00:43:40.580
of opioids, synthetic
compounds like heroin,

00:43:40.580 --> 00:43:43.460
sometimes mistakenly
called China white.

00:43:43.460 --> 00:43:48.440
China white is really real
heroin, a very pure kind.

00:43:48.440 --> 00:43:50.720
But these
fentanyl-like compounds

00:43:50.720 --> 00:43:53.150
are synthetic analogs.

00:43:53.150 --> 00:43:55.910
And there's work going
on now to try and develop

00:43:55.910 --> 00:43:58.070
these as weapons.

00:43:58.070 --> 00:44:00.710
The idea would be
to release them

00:44:00.710 --> 00:44:04.250
as an aerosol and people
would be knocked out.

00:44:04.250 --> 00:44:06.710
A problem with this is
that, although these

00:44:06.710 --> 00:44:10.160
are the kinds of compounds that
are used in darts to capture

00:44:10.160 --> 00:44:14.590
wild animals, it doesn't
work on primates because they

00:44:14.590 --> 00:44:19.220
are more sensitive to
the respiratory arrest

00:44:19.220 --> 00:44:21.620
properties of these
fentanyl compounds,

00:44:21.620 --> 00:44:23.330
these synthetic opioids.

00:44:23.330 --> 00:44:24.860
And so they're
lethal, too often.

00:44:24.860 --> 00:44:28.410
They would be lethal too often
in practice for primates,

00:44:28.410 --> 00:44:30.470
including human primates.

00:44:30.470 --> 00:44:33.560
And so there's research
trying to mix them

00:44:33.560 --> 00:44:36.050
with antidotes against
their respiratory effects,

00:44:36.050 --> 00:44:38.600
but to maintain their
knockout effects.

00:44:38.600 --> 00:44:41.180
And one has to agree that
there are military situations

00:44:41.180 --> 00:44:44.300
in which one side or
another might use such

00:44:44.300 --> 00:44:47.800
a weapon to some advantage.

00:44:47.800 --> 00:44:53.500
The treaty protects all peaceful
purposes and police purposes

00:44:53.500 --> 00:44:55.570
and riot control
purposes of chemicals.

00:44:55.570 --> 00:44:57.040
That's not at issue.

00:44:57.040 --> 00:44:59.950
What is at issue is the
United States policy

00:44:59.950 --> 00:45:02.020
which, at the moment at least--

00:45:02.020 --> 00:45:05.560
and our negotiators in Geneva
will tell you that this policy

00:45:05.560 --> 00:45:08.330
is absolutely non-negotiable
unless the president himself

00:45:08.330 --> 00:45:10.280
changes it--

00:45:10.280 --> 00:45:13.910
insists that if a chemical
is used for domestic law

00:45:13.910 --> 00:45:16.610
enforcement by a state
party, then that chemical

00:45:16.610 --> 00:45:18.365
may be used freely
without the restraints.

00:45:18.365 --> 00:45:21.150
And which we now, by the
way, do impose ourselves.

00:45:21.150 --> 00:45:24.510
Those restraints would be gone.

00:45:24.510 --> 00:45:27.330
That chemical could
be used freely in war.

00:45:27.330 --> 00:45:29.310
That, I think, could
be a big mistake

00:45:29.310 --> 00:45:31.680
because it's a loophole.

00:45:31.680 --> 00:45:35.240
Now, an incapacitating gas is
a long way from a lethal one,

00:45:35.240 --> 00:45:38.050
and it's some distance away
from a biological agent,

00:45:38.050 --> 00:45:40.880
and that's some distance
away from a lethal biological

00:45:40.880 --> 00:45:42.800
infectious agent.

00:45:42.800 --> 00:45:45.170
But both the pattern of
proliferation in the world

00:45:45.170 --> 00:45:49.100
and in the thinking
of many people,

00:45:49.100 --> 00:45:53.120
there is a kind of linkage
amongst all these infectious

00:45:53.120 --> 00:45:54.260
and toxic--

00:45:54.260 --> 00:45:57.140
all these things which take
advantage of the inner life

00:45:57.140 --> 00:45:59.210
processes, all
these things which

00:45:59.210 --> 00:46:04.250
could be developed and perfected
over time by deeper knowledge

00:46:04.250 --> 00:46:07.100
of our own living systems.

00:46:07.100 --> 00:46:09.700
People tend to think
of these as a unit.

00:46:09.700 --> 00:46:11.510
And I don't know if
fine distinctions would

00:46:11.510 --> 00:46:14.480
last very long if one
nation or another insisted

00:46:14.480 --> 00:46:16.820
on using just that end
of the spectrum that

00:46:16.820 --> 00:46:19.110
was useful for it.

00:46:19.110 --> 00:46:22.620
Especially if the other nations
have to be dragged along

00:46:22.620 --> 00:46:23.740
to make that distinction.

00:46:23.740 --> 00:46:25.007
So I hope that doesn't happen.

00:46:27.990 --> 00:46:29.880
So I've really come
to the end, and I hope

00:46:29.880 --> 00:46:32.280
I have left time for questions.

00:46:32.280 --> 00:46:35.600
I'd like to end by saying,
and really repeating

00:46:35.600 --> 00:46:40.450
what I said before,
that it might be wise

00:46:40.450 --> 00:46:44.660
now at the very beginning of
the real revolution in biology

00:46:44.660 --> 00:46:49.610
just to put a hold on all the
hostile uses of our relatively

00:46:49.610 --> 00:46:51.790
new science.

00:46:51.790 --> 00:46:53.740
We don't lose much
by doing that,

00:46:53.740 --> 00:46:56.020
and we might gain a great deal.

00:46:56.020 --> 00:46:57.242
Thank you.

00:46:57.242 --> 00:46:59.697
[APPLAUSE]

00:47:05.589 --> 00:47:06.571
PRESENTER: [INAUDIBLE]

00:47:06.571 --> 00:47:07.062
MATTHEW MESELSON: Yeah.

00:47:07.062 --> 00:47:07.562
Good.

00:47:21.330 --> 00:47:27.250
PRESENTER: Our discussant
for Matt's talk

00:47:27.250 --> 00:47:33.280
is John Deutch, who's served
as MIT provost from 1985

00:47:33.280 --> 00:47:39.160
until 1990.

00:47:39.160 --> 00:47:43.570
He's currently Institute
Professor here at MIT.

00:47:43.570 --> 00:47:53.190
In August of 1990 George
Bush appointed John Deutch

00:47:53.190 --> 00:47:57.330
to the president's Foreign
Intelligence Advisory Board.

00:47:57.330 --> 00:48:00.690
He's also served on the Defense
Policy Board of the Defense

00:48:00.690 --> 00:48:01.410
Science Board.

00:48:04.250 --> 00:48:09.760
You can read a great deal of his
public service in these areas

00:48:09.760 --> 00:48:12.570
in the biography here.

00:48:16.084 --> 00:48:17.500
I was particularly
anxious to have

00:48:17.500 --> 00:48:22.750
John be here because Matt
is such a compelling speaker

00:48:22.750 --> 00:48:26.790
that I always feel,
after hearing him,

00:48:26.790 --> 00:48:30.390
that I now agree completely
with what he's said.

00:48:30.390 --> 00:48:35.520
And whenever I get into a
situation of thinking I really

00:48:35.520 --> 00:48:39.630
know what the right course
is, I talk to John Deutch

00:48:39.630 --> 00:48:44.280
and he convinces me that
that's completely wrong.

00:48:44.280 --> 00:48:48.660
So I think John is in the
spirit of the Henry Kissinger's

00:48:48.660 --> 00:48:50.210
options.

00:48:50.210 --> 00:48:53.190
And in that spirit of making
us think about something

00:48:53.190 --> 00:48:57.210
else, other
possibilities, I'm very

00:48:57.210 --> 00:48:59.623
happy to welcome John
as the discussant.

00:48:59.623 --> 00:49:02.088
[APPLAUSE]

00:49:17.520 --> 00:49:18.770
JOHN DEUTCH: Thank you, Jerry.

00:49:18.770 --> 00:49:23.930
I'm pleased to learn that I
was invited to participate

00:49:23.930 --> 00:49:26.810
this evening because
perhaps I could offer

00:49:26.810 --> 00:49:32.000
some interesting views
in contrast to the fact

00:49:32.000 --> 00:49:34.190
that I'm not a biologist
and would satisfy

00:49:34.190 --> 00:49:37.716
the terms of the grant
under which these--

00:49:37.716 --> 00:49:40.150
[LAUGHTER]

00:49:40.650 --> 00:49:44.330
I'm also very pleased to
appear at the same time as Matt

00:49:44.330 --> 00:49:46.190
Meselson.

00:49:46.190 --> 00:49:48.740
Matt has dedicated
himself to study

00:49:48.740 --> 00:49:51.590
of the subjects of biological
and chemical warfare

00:49:51.590 --> 00:49:56.180
as he's described, I think,
scrupulously and very carefully

00:49:56.180 --> 00:49:59.790
for, I guess,
about 30 years now.

00:49:59.790 --> 00:50:05.540
And that serves the public well,
and we have every great reason

00:50:05.540 --> 00:50:09.537
to listen to him carefully
and to be pleased

00:50:09.537 --> 00:50:11.120
that there's somebody
in our community

00:50:11.120 --> 00:50:15.700
who is taking the pains to
follow such important matters.

00:50:15.700 --> 00:50:18.640
I don't believe that
I'm going to stand here

00:50:18.640 --> 00:50:21.220
before you with a
long list of items

00:50:21.220 --> 00:50:27.040
on which I disagree about the
subject which Matt has dressed.

00:50:27.040 --> 00:50:28.780
What I do want to
do is perhaps spend

00:50:28.780 --> 00:50:30.670
a moment touching on
a few of the points

00:50:30.670 --> 00:50:33.040
which were brought up.

00:50:33.040 --> 00:50:37.150
Let me begin though with
bringing you some bad news

00:50:37.150 --> 00:50:39.930
and some good news.

00:50:39.930 --> 00:50:45.030
The bad news is that I believe
that the world is entering

00:50:45.030 --> 00:50:50.760
a period where a proliferation
of weapons of mass destruction

00:50:50.760 --> 00:50:53.690
is going to be of
significantly larger concern

00:50:53.690 --> 00:50:58.230
than it has been in the past
several decades, where we've

00:50:58.230 --> 00:51:02.400
been really moderately fortunate
at the slow pace at which

00:51:02.400 --> 00:51:05.710
weapons of mass destruction
have diffused throughout nations

00:51:05.710 --> 00:51:06.896
of the world.

00:51:06.896 --> 00:51:08.510
There are a lot of
reasons for that.

00:51:08.510 --> 00:51:09.760
I don't want to dwell on them.

00:51:09.760 --> 00:51:11.920
I think it's partially,
but not entirely,

00:51:11.920 --> 00:51:16.720
connected with the collapse
of the former Soviet Union.

00:51:16.720 --> 00:51:19.120
But there is also a growing
regional instability

00:51:19.120 --> 00:51:23.440
in the world which
leads countries to seek,

00:51:23.440 --> 00:51:25.780
unfortunately, weapons
of mass destruction,

00:51:25.780 --> 00:51:27.850
quite independent
of what the United

00:51:27.850 --> 00:51:34.680
States, the former Soviet Union,
or other European or Japan--

00:51:34.680 --> 00:51:37.050
independently of what they do.

00:51:37.050 --> 00:51:39.340
Now, these weapons
of mass destruction

00:51:39.340 --> 00:51:41.240
are three varieties.

00:51:41.240 --> 00:51:44.340
There are nuclear
weapons, chemical weapons,

00:51:44.340 --> 00:51:47.800
and the biological weapons
of which Matt principally

00:51:47.800 --> 00:51:48.790
addressed.

00:51:48.790 --> 00:51:50.000
That's the bad news.

00:51:50.000 --> 00:51:53.180
So I think it's a very,
very serious problem.

00:51:53.180 --> 00:51:55.510
It a principal
problem, I would say,

00:51:55.510 --> 00:51:58.000
that should be on the foreign
policy agenda of the United

00:51:58.000 --> 00:52:02.020
States currently,
with the happy loss

00:52:02.020 --> 00:52:05.710
of the political competition
with the Soviet Union.

00:52:05.710 --> 00:52:07.010
That is really bad news.

00:52:07.010 --> 00:52:10.600
And I would like to spend
some time mentioning

00:52:10.600 --> 00:52:13.990
to you, talking to you about
this issue of proliferation.

00:52:13.990 --> 00:52:16.090
The good news is,
for those of you

00:52:16.090 --> 00:52:19.580
who are rather more concered
with the principal topic

00:52:19.580 --> 00:52:22.900
of this evening's
conversation, that I just

00:52:22.900 --> 00:52:24.910
don't think modern
biology is going

00:52:24.910 --> 00:52:29.560
to be a central player in
this for some period of time.

00:52:29.560 --> 00:52:32.060
And I think on that
Matt and I share,

00:52:32.060 --> 00:52:35.500
that you just don't need
a lot better or worse

00:52:35.500 --> 00:52:36.940
than what we already have.

00:52:36.940 --> 00:52:39.970
Slightly better
living organisms still

00:52:39.970 --> 00:52:43.480
are inconvenient to
manage and handle,

00:52:43.480 --> 00:52:46.330
and not militarily
useful in my view,

00:52:46.330 --> 00:52:49.390
and there's really no
purpose in developing them.

00:52:49.390 --> 00:52:54.370
Complex toxins developed maybe
by biotechnology techniques

00:52:54.370 --> 00:52:57.340
or genetic engineering
in the future strike me

00:52:57.340 --> 00:53:01.780
as still having to
compete with the rather

00:53:01.780 --> 00:53:05.980
awesome organophosphate
nerve agents which

00:53:05.980 --> 00:53:09.010
are present in quantity
and easy to produce.

00:53:09.010 --> 00:53:13.090
Odorless, colorless, and have
the ability to survive, and--

00:53:13.090 --> 00:53:15.830
some of them have the ability
to survive in some climates.

00:53:15.830 --> 00:53:19.360
So I really do not believe
that we run the risk here,

00:53:19.360 --> 00:53:20.800
as we do perhaps
in other subjects

00:53:20.800 --> 00:53:22.640
that this symposium
will be addressing,

00:53:22.640 --> 00:53:26.400
or the seminar will be
addressing on future evenings,

00:53:26.400 --> 00:53:28.210
of saying that
molecular biology has

00:53:28.210 --> 00:53:32.630
a particular-- or modern biology
has a particular problem here.

00:53:32.630 --> 00:53:35.740
But I do think that we
have jointly at issue,

00:53:35.740 --> 00:53:38.560
whether you call it
new biology or not,

00:53:38.560 --> 00:53:44.140
to worry about the proliferation
of nuclear, chemical weapons

00:53:44.140 --> 00:53:45.790
of mass destruction.

00:53:45.790 --> 00:53:47.800
Especially in
regions of the world

00:53:47.800 --> 00:53:51.310
that are highly unstable
and prone to violence.

00:53:54.800 --> 00:53:59.300
When I look at the lesson
of Iraq, the war in Iraq

00:53:59.300 --> 00:54:02.660
as it bears on this
subject, I find

00:54:02.660 --> 00:54:07.580
that there are three
points of great importance.

00:54:07.580 --> 00:54:11.540
First, I think that it is
shocking and scandalous that

00:54:11.540 --> 00:54:15.590
the world stood by while there
was an all-out war between Iran

00:54:15.590 --> 00:54:20.000
and Iraq and saw the fairly
widespread use of chemicals

00:54:20.000 --> 00:54:23.810
in that war causing
really substantial numbers

00:54:23.810 --> 00:54:28.130
of fatalities and
injuries and in no way--

00:54:28.130 --> 00:54:31.730
in no diplomatic way, much
less any more forceful means--

00:54:31.730 --> 00:54:35.150
intervened in that conflict.

00:54:35.150 --> 00:54:38.060
I think that the
absence of intervention

00:54:38.060 --> 00:54:42.560
in the Iraq-Iran war in the
matter of the use of chemicals

00:54:42.560 --> 00:54:46.940
did indeed provide a lesson,
an unfortunate lesson,

00:54:46.940 --> 00:54:51.350
for nations of the world that
I might regard as rogue states.

00:54:51.350 --> 00:54:55.860
It gave them a greater interest
in weapons of mass destruction.

00:54:55.860 --> 00:54:59.910
Second point I would like to
make about the Iran-Iraq war

00:54:59.910 --> 00:55:04.920
has to do with the enormous
error of US intelligence

00:55:04.920 --> 00:55:06.840
or US policy with
respect to what

00:55:06.840 --> 00:55:09.180
was going on in Iran and Iraq.

00:55:09.180 --> 00:55:12.720
And indeed, the era occurred
with opposite [INAUDIBLE]

00:55:12.720 --> 00:55:14.370
in the case of nuclear
and in the case

00:55:14.370 --> 00:55:16.660
of chemical and biological.

00:55:16.660 --> 00:55:20.815
In the case of nuclear weapons
the estimates before the war--

00:55:20.815 --> 00:55:22.670
and, in fact, during the war--

00:55:22.670 --> 00:55:24.560
were that Iraq had--

00:55:24.560 --> 00:55:28.250
was a signatory, I might add,
of the nonproliferation treaty--

00:55:28.250 --> 00:55:31.210
Iraq was a signatory of the
nonproliferation treaty,

00:55:31.210 --> 00:55:33.860
and whose one declared
nuclear facility

00:55:33.860 --> 00:55:36.440
was indeed inspected
and properly inspected

00:55:36.440 --> 00:55:39.180
by the International
Atomic Energy Agency.

00:55:39.180 --> 00:55:41.420
But the official estimate
and the policy view

00:55:41.420 --> 00:55:44.270
was that, while they certainly
had a covert nuclear weapons

00:55:44.270 --> 00:55:51.010
program that it was R&amp;D,
modest in scale, and so forth.

00:55:51.010 --> 00:55:53.710
Point of fact it is now
quite completely apparent

00:55:53.710 --> 00:55:55.049
with the quite successful--

00:55:55.049 --> 00:55:56.590
I guess it had been
10 up until now--

00:55:56.590 --> 00:55:58.960
IAEA inspections of Iraq.

00:55:58.960 --> 00:56:01.270
They had an absolutely
massive program from soup

00:56:01.270 --> 00:56:03.910
to nuts from mining
ore, all the way

00:56:03.910 --> 00:56:09.310
to experimenting with
sophisticated means

00:56:09.310 --> 00:56:13.780
of enriching uranium to
obtain highly-enriched uranium

00:56:13.780 --> 00:56:15.100
for bomb use.

00:56:15.100 --> 00:56:18.370
They had somewhere
between 10 and 15,000

00:56:18.370 --> 00:56:21.730
trained scientists and engineers
working in this program.

00:56:21.730 --> 00:56:24.280
It was not a little side bit.

00:56:24.280 --> 00:56:28.090
It was an enormous venture,
which completely escaped

00:56:28.090 --> 00:56:30.760
reporting not only
of the United States

00:56:30.760 --> 00:56:33.160
and its intelligence
services, but similarly

00:56:33.160 --> 00:56:35.170
the reporting of
the intelligence

00:56:35.170 --> 00:56:38.770
services of the French,
the British, the Russians,

00:56:38.770 --> 00:56:41.620
and the Israelis to the
extent that you can believe

00:56:41.620 --> 00:56:43.960
the information they provide.

00:56:43.960 --> 00:56:47.020
In that case, we had an
enormous underestimate

00:56:47.020 --> 00:56:52.300
of what was going on in Iraq,
a country which at that time

00:56:52.300 --> 00:56:53.940
had a major nuclear
weapons program.

00:56:53.940 --> 00:56:55.648
I do not want to
suggest to you that they

00:56:55.648 --> 00:56:57.580
were eight months or
six months or five days

00:56:57.580 --> 00:56:58.964
away from having a bomb.

00:56:58.964 --> 00:57:00.380
What I do want to
say is that they

00:57:00.380 --> 00:57:02.180
had a very serious program.

00:57:02.180 --> 00:57:04.330
We have at this very
moment a similar discussion

00:57:04.330 --> 00:57:06.030
going on with North Korea.

00:57:06.030 --> 00:57:08.810
There is great concern about
what is happening in Algeria.

00:57:08.810 --> 00:57:10.810
There's concern about
what is happening in Iran,

00:57:10.810 --> 00:57:13.240
and there is concern about
what is happening, certainly,

00:57:13.240 --> 00:57:15.130
in Libya.

00:57:15.130 --> 00:57:17.800
On that side, we
underestimated dramatically

00:57:17.800 --> 00:57:20.850
what was going on
in nuclear matters

00:57:20.850 --> 00:57:24.040
in the case of a nuclear
proliferation treaty

00:57:24.040 --> 00:57:27.560
signatory in Iraq.

00:57:27.560 --> 00:57:30.350
On the chemical
side we estimated--

00:57:30.350 --> 00:57:34.990
we estimated-- that the Iraqis
had deliverable chemicals

00:57:34.990 --> 00:57:37.950
in a variety of forms,
including on scud missiles,

00:57:37.950 --> 00:57:40.500
and that if the
United States forces--

00:57:40.500 --> 00:57:42.980
if United States forces
joined with them in the desert

00:57:42.980 --> 00:57:45.350
that they would
use these chemical

00:57:45.350 --> 00:57:47.850
agents against US forces.

00:57:47.850 --> 00:57:50.930
Matt has described to
you, quite correctly, it

00:57:50.930 --> 00:57:54.920
was also a concern that they
had biological agent, anthrax

00:57:54.920 --> 00:57:58.400
and botulinum, that this
would be potentially used

00:57:58.400 --> 00:57:59.630
against US servicemen.

00:57:59.630 --> 00:58:02.240
And there was a
desultory program

00:58:02.240 --> 00:58:06.760
to inoculate them
during the campaign.

00:58:06.760 --> 00:58:09.660
It turned out, of course,
that the Iraqis did not

00:58:09.660 --> 00:58:11.430
use their chemical weapons.

00:58:11.430 --> 00:58:15.810
Indeed it is a striking feature
that Mr. Saddam Hussein,

00:58:15.810 --> 00:58:19.980
to best of my knowledge, did not
deploy any chemical munition--

00:58:19.980 --> 00:58:21.450
of which we had many.

00:58:21.450 --> 00:58:26.310
And we now know that he had
at least 30 scud warheads that

00:58:26.310 --> 00:58:28.760
were available to be used,
including binary agent

00:58:28.760 --> 00:58:30.350
warheads--

00:58:30.350 --> 00:58:33.570
that he did not deploy any
of these chemical weapons

00:58:33.570 --> 00:58:35.820
into the Kuwaiti
theater of operations

00:58:35.820 --> 00:58:39.150
or anywhere near Basra
or south of Basra.

00:58:39.150 --> 00:58:44.130
And that therefore he was much
more reluctant or unable--

00:58:44.130 --> 00:58:45.900
to argue it either
way, but certainly

00:58:45.900 --> 00:58:47.970
there was some initial
reluctance of him

00:58:47.970 --> 00:58:49.650
to deploy these weapons--

00:58:49.650 --> 00:58:52.080
so that in this case
there was an instance

00:58:52.080 --> 00:58:55.980
of enormous, enormous
overestimating

00:58:55.980 --> 00:58:58.850
how likely chemical
weapons were to be used.

00:58:58.850 --> 00:59:01.650
The point I want to make is not
to argue the rights or wrongs

00:59:01.650 --> 00:59:04.440
of either one of those
pictures, but to indicate

00:59:04.440 --> 00:59:07.800
how absolutely difficult
it is going to be,

00:59:07.800 --> 00:59:10.140
in a time when we
are trying to slow

00:59:10.140 --> 00:59:13.050
the proliferation of these
weapons of mass destruction,

00:59:13.050 --> 00:59:16.980
to assess properly
and at a policy level

00:59:16.980 --> 00:59:19.020
what the picture is
before one intervenes

00:59:19.020 --> 00:59:20.350
in a diplomatic matter.

00:59:20.350 --> 00:59:22.190
Especially if one
wants to intervene

00:59:22.190 --> 00:59:26.010
in a diplomatic matter in
concert with other nations,

00:59:26.010 --> 00:59:29.424
perhaps through the UN, perhaps
through some other agency

00:59:29.424 --> 00:59:30.340
in a matter like this.

00:59:30.340 --> 00:59:33.690
And, as I say, the matter is
presently an identical case

00:59:33.690 --> 00:59:36.070
occurs in North
Korea where we have

00:59:36.070 --> 00:59:40.710
astonishing little information
about what is influencing

00:59:40.710 --> 00:59:42.373
the North Korean government.

00:59:45.760 --> 00:59:47.320
The reason I raise
this is that I

00:59:47.320 --> 00:59:49.360
would like to conclude
by making a comment

00:59:49.360 --> 00:59:51.880
about the multilateral
chemical treaty.

00:59:51.880 --> 00:59:56.160
It is indeed the
most important--

00:59:56.160 --> 00:59:58.800
the most significant, I should
say-- the most significant arms

00:59:58.800 --> 01:00:02.130
control measure in this
area that is on the agenda,

01:00:02.130 --> 01:00:05.130
I would say, for the
next five or 10 years.

01:00:05.130 --> 01:00:10.560
For a variety of reasons the
multilateral chemical treaty

01:00:10.560 --> 01:00:12.300
is a treaty which
is of great interest

01:00:12.300 --> 01:00:14.010
to this administration.

01:00:14.010 --> 01:00:15.900
It has the support
of the president.

01:00:15.900 --> 01:00:19.740
And I would bet
more than even money

01:00:19.740 --> 01:00:22.500
that that treaty will be passed.

01:00:22.500 --> 01:00:25.500
What concerns me about that
treaty is the following.

01:00:25.500 --> 01:00:27.360
While it is a measure
that I support--

01:00:27.360 --> 01:00:30.480
I think its a reasonable
thing for the United States

01:00:30.480 --> 01:00:34.170
to enter into and to
encourage within the community

01:00:34.170 --> 01:00:35.130
of nations--

01:00:35.130 --> 01:00:39.270
it does have several very,
very severe weaknesses.

01:00:39.270 --> 01:00:42.125
And I mention them
only to you in passing.

01:00:42.125 --> 01:00:44.060
The first is that
there is really

01:00:44.060 --> 01:00:45.500
an issue of verification.

01:00:45.500 --> 01:00:48.480
It is extremely difficult-- as
I've tried to illustrate with

01:00:48.480 --> 01:00:50.490
my remarks about Iraq--

01:00:50.490 --> 01:00:53.940
very, very difficult to know
that one has an accurate

01:00:53.940 --> 01:00:57.650
picture, even with very
sophisticated intelligence

01:00:57.650 --> 01:01:00.690
gathering and other information
gathering techniques.

01:01:00.690 --> 01:01:03.300
"Very sophisticated"
means it is very difficult

01:01:03.300 --> 01:01:05.190
to know what is
going on, especially

01:01:05.190 --> 01:01:08.640
covertly in a nation.

01:01:08.640 --> 01:01:10.620
Not a friendly
nation, but a nation

01:01:10.620 --> 01:01:12.810
that sees itself having
security concerns

01:01:12.810 --> 01:01:17.130
and maybe having less
than friendly intentions

01:01:17.130 --> 01:01:18.930
towards its neighbor.

01:01:18.930 --> 01:01:22.880
Second is that that treaty does
not really, as I understand it,

01:01:22.880 --> 01:01:27.150
contain a "no first
use" declaration by all

01:01:27.150 --> 01:01:27.840
of its members.

01:01:27.840 --> 01:01:29.550
I believe it should.

01:01:29.550 --> 01:01:32.860
Nor does the treaty agree--

01:01:32.860 --> 01:01:36.640
nor does the treaty agree
to what the signatories will

01:01:36.640 --> 01:01:41.680
do if somebody either
violates the treaty

01:01:41.680 --> 01:01:44.740
or proceeds to use chemicals
even if they are not

01:01:44.740 --> 01:01:47.070
members of the treaty.

01:01:47.070 --> 01:01:50.250
And until the issue
of sanctions is

01:01:50.250 --> 01:01:53.540
agreed upon by those
people who enter

01:01:53.540 --> 01:01:56.180
into the multilateral
chemical treaty,

01:01:56.180 --> 01:01:58.280
I think that we will
have stopped short

01:01:58.280 --> 01:02:00.980
of taking a
multilateral step that

01:02:00.980 --> 01:02:05.990
will indeed effectively slow
the spread of chemical weapons.

01:02:05.990 --> 01:02:08.750
Because as matters
stand now the world

01:02:08.750 --> 01:02:11.526
has the lesson,
first, of Iran-Iraq,

01:02:11.526 --> 01:02:14.750
a war where chemicals
were freely used,

01:02:14.750 --> 01:02:18.140
and everybody stood by.

01:02:18.140 --> 01:02:22.610
And the second, we have
the example of the Iraq war

01:02:22.610 --> 01:02:26.450
itself, the most recent
conflict in the Gulf

01:02:26.450 --> 01:02:28.280
where, indeed, we
had a signatory

01:02:28.280 --> 01:02:30.200
to the nonproliferation
treaty who

01:02:30.200 --> 01:02:35.360
was violating the nuclear
part of the treaty--

01:02:35.360 --> 01:02:37.820
the Nuclear Nonproliferation
Treaty-- and was not

01:02:37.820 --> 01:02:42.020
being brought to bear for it
by any inspection scheme that

01:02:42.020 --> 01:02:44.860
was present.

01:02:44.860 --> 01:02:48.400
In sum, the multilateral
treaty, in my judgment,

01:02:48.400 --> 01:02:51.622
needs to have a
strengthening along the lines

01:02:51.622 --> 01:02:52.330
that I mentioned.

01:02:52.330 --> 01:02:56.530
No first use, some discussion
and agreement among its members

01:02:56.530 --> 01:03:00.190
about what sanctions will
be taken in case someone

01:03:00.190 --> 01:03:02.200
does violate that treaty.

01:03:02.200 --> 01:03:04.960
Why doesn't that take place?

01:03:04.960 --> 01:03:06.280
Why doesn't that take place?

01:03:06.280 --> 01:03:08.910
The reason that there is
not such an agreement,

01:03:08.910 --> 01:03:11.430
why that is not included in
the treaty in my judgment,

01:03:11.430 --> 01:03:16.140
is that policy makers today
in the United States--

01:03:16.140 --> 01:03:20.280
in Washington, or elsewhere
across the globe--

01:03:20.280 --> 01:03:23.670
face two questions, and
have no good answers

01:03:23.670 --> 01:03:25.420
to those questions.

01:03:25.420 --> 01:03:28.980
The first question is, how
do you deter proliferation?

01:03:28.980 --> 01:03:30.830
How do you slow the spread?

01:03:30.830 --> 01:03:34.590
And I must say that I think
since 1974, the last time we

01:03:34.590 --> 01:03:37.500
had, for example, a
detonation of a nuclear weapon

01:03:37.500 --> 01:03:39.780
by a country, India,
there are probably

01:03:39.780 --> 01:03:42.420
three new members
of the nuclear club.

01:03:42.420 --> 01:03:46.560
Certainly Israel, South
Africa, and Pakistan.

01:03:46.560 --> 01:03:48.614
We don't know how
to slow that spread.

01:03:48.614 --> 01:03:51.030
We don't know how to slow that
spread in the nuclear area.

01:03:51.030 --> 01:03:54.240
Much more difficult in the
chemical and biological area.

01:03:54.240 --> 01:03:57.660
And secondly, if we
arrive at a situation

01:03:57.660 --> 01:03:59.430
where somebody does
proliferate, we

01:03:59.430 --> 01:04:01.770
have no effective
policy measures

01:04:01.770 --> 01:04:05.026
of knowing what to do about
it in a political manner.

01:04:05.026 --> 01:04:06.970
Thank you very much.

01:04:06.970 --> 01:04:09.400
[APPLAUSE]

01:04:12.320 --> 01:04:14.431
MODERATOR: Why don't
you take a seat up here?

01:04:14.431 --> 01:04:14.860
JOHN DEUTCH: With Matt.

01:04:14.860 --> 01:04:15.735
MODERATOR: With Matt.

01:04:15.735 --> 01:04:16.260
Yes.

01:04:16.260 --> 01:04:18.470
If I can ask both
of our speakers

01:04:18.470 --> 01:04:20.450
to come up to the front,
I'm sure that there

01:04:20.450 --> 01:04:23.520
are many questions
from the audience.

01:04:23.520 --> 01:04:25.390
I know some people will
need to leave there,

01:04:25.390 --> 01:04:27.620
but the speakers have
agreed to stay on

01:04:27.620 --> 01:04:30.750
for about 10 or 15
minutes of questions.

01:04:30.750 --> 01:04:34.100
We have a microphone down here
for those who want to use it.

01:04:34.100 --> 01:04:37.167
For those with loud voices,
I'm willing to recognize people

01:04:37.167 --> 01:04:38.750
anywhere in the
audience if they don't

01:04:38.750 --> 01:04:39.890
want to come down to use it.

01:04:39.890 --> 01:04:41.390
There's a first
question over there.

01:04:44.750 --> 01:04:47.740
Oh, and could I ask you--
because this is such a diverse

01:04:47.740 --> 01:04:49.132
audience we've
tried to attract--

01:04:49.132 --> 01:04:50.840
to say who you are
and where you're from?

01:04:50.840 --> 01:04:52.465
Because I think it
would be nice for us

01:04:52.465 --> 01:04:54.834
to know the range of
people who have come today.

01:04:54.834 --> 01:04:57.219
AUDIENCE: Howard Hugh, a
physician epidemiologist

01:04:57.219 --> 01:04:59.604
at [INAUDIBLE] School
of Public Health.

01:04:59.604 --> 01:05:02.000
First question for
Professor Deutch.

01:05:02.000 --> 01:05:04.963
I wonder if you'd
comment on the point

01:05:04.963 --> 01:05:07.845
that Professor
Meselson raised, which

01:05:07.845 --> 01:05:12.853
is that the US continues to try
to exclude riot control agents

01:05:12.853 --> 01:05:15.440
from the chemical weapons ban.

01:05:15.440 --> 01:05:19.640
It seems, for one thing, that
riot control agents are not

01:05:19.640 --> 01:05:26.410
quite as non-lethal as the US
would like to portray them.

01:05:26.410 --> 01:05:28.600
They are lethal in high
enough concentrations,

01:05:28.600 --> 01:05:31.818
and their toxicity in
terms of chronic effects

01:05:31.818 --> 01:05:35.722
has been not
adequately addressed.

01:05:35.722 --> 01:05:38.490
And it would put chemical
weapons and the US position

01:05:38.490 --> 01:05:42.800
on the so-called "slippery
slope" of advocating

01:05:42.800 --> 01:05:45.952
toxic versus non-toxic weapons.

01:05:45.952 --> 01:05:50.210
The second question
is that, it's clear

01:05:50.210 --> 01:05:56.000
that Iraq didn't use its
chemical weapons partly

01:05:56.000 --> 01:05:58.934
because the US
admittedly had over 500

01:05:58.934 --> 01:06:02.950
tactical nuclear weapons in the
Mid-East theater of operations,

01:06:02.950 --> 01:06:05.430
and it wouldn't quite
make sense to use

01:06:05.430 --> 01:06:08.110
one weapon of mass destruction
and be totally wiped

01:06:08.110 --> 01:06:10.160
out by a second.

01:06:10.160 --> 01:06:13.054
But it would seem that
chemical and biological weapons

01:06:13.054 --> 01:06:16.390
are a much greater threat
in the developing world--

01:06:16.390 --> 01:06:19.060
developing nations against
developing nations.

01:06:19.060 --> 01:06:21.686
What divisions might
there be for looking

01:06:21.686 --> 01:06:25.430
at those kinds of issues?

01:06:25.430 --> 01:06:28.970
JOHN DEUTCH: Well, I must admit
to a scholarly deficiency.

01:06:28.970 --> 01:06:33.070
I have not kept so close
to the BW convention

01:06:33.070 --> 01:06:36.010
and the subsequent negotiations
to know what could even

01:06:36.010 --> 01:06:38.710
be described
[INAUDIBLE] reliably

01:06:38.710 --> 01:06:43.490
why our position is so strong
on incapacitated agents.

01:06:43.490 --> 01:06:45.670
The apocryphal story
in the past was

01:06:45.670 --> 01:06:48.970
that the police
chief of New Orleans

01:06:48.970 --> 01:06:51.410
was really insistent
on keeping BZ--

01:06:51.410 --> 01:06:55.900
I guess is what it
was-- which is tear gas.

01:06:55.900 --> 01:06:57.709
Or, is BZ tear gas?

01:06:57.709 --> 01:06:58.750
MATTHEW MESELSON: No, CS.

01:06:58.750 --> 01:07:00.910
JOHN DEUTCH: CS is tear gas--

01:07:00.910 --> 01:07:03.490
available for domestic use.

01:07:03.490 --> 01:07:05.080
And at that time
there was a confusion

01:07:05.080 --> 01:07:06.454
about if you used
it domestically

01:07:06.454 --> 01:07:09.940
for civilian control purposes
whether you would have a right

01:07:09.940 --> 01:07:11.030
to use it in the military.

01:07:11.030 --> 01:07:14.350
But I want to tell you, this
is not a big deal with me.

01:07:14.350 --> 01:07:16.780
And I would guess that if
somebody put their mind to it

01:07:16.780 --> 01:07:17.830
we could probably change that.

01:07:17.830 --> 01:07:18.970
We could probably get the
position of the United

01:07:18.970 --> 01:07:19.760
States changed on it.

01:07:19.760 --> 01:07:21.010
I don't think it's a big deal.

01:07:21.010 --> 01:07:23.560
I can't see where
in the government

01:07:23.560 --> 01:07:27.242
there's going to be a real
hard overview on this.

01:07:27.242 --> 01:07:28.450
So to me it's not a big deal.

01:07:28.450 --> 01:07:31.180
And apocryphally it's
because of the police chief

01:07:31.180 --> 01:07:32.260
of New Orleans.

01:07:32.260 --> 01:07:34.250
That's the answer to
the first question.

01:07:34.250 --> 01:07:37.960
Second question, I think, is
a little bit more complicated.

01:07:37.960 --> 01:07:40.860
I do not believe it would be in
the United States's interests.

01:07:40.860 --> 01:07:44.440
And I believe the president
declared this quite clearly,

01:07:44.440 --> 01:07:48.340
that if chemicals had been
used against US forces

01:07:48.340 --> 01:07:50.980
in the Middle East
I would not believe

01:07:50.980 --> 01:07:53.170
that the president would
have or should have used

01:07:53.170 --> 01:07:56.537
nuclear weapons in response.

01:07:56.537 --> 01:07:58.120
You might say to me,
it doesn't matter

01:07:58.120 --> 01:08:00.040
what you think or
believe, it's what

01:08:00.040 --> 01:08:01.360
Saddam Hussein [INAUDIBLE].

01:08:01.360 --> 01:08:02.680
[LAUGHTER]

01:08:02.680 --> 01:08:03.180
But I do--

01:08:03.180 --> 01:08:05.638
I think-- I want to tell you
that the issue with the United

01:08:05.638 --> 01:08:07.960
States is a little bit
more dicey, because I think

01:08:07.960 --> 01:08:12.070
we have to worry
about how we deter use

01:08:12.070 --> 01:08:15.460
against our own forces
overseas, or our own cities,

01:08:15.460 --> 01:08:19.405
if you like, but not say the
way we deter it is, I think,

01:08:19.405 --> 01:08:22.382
is mindless and, quite frankly,
not quite credible, to say,

01:08:22.382 --> 01:08:23.840
we'll hit you with
nuclear weapons.

01:08:23.840 --> 01:08:26.120
I don't think that's an
adequate response for us.

01:08:26.120 --> 01:08:28.840
On the other hand, I'm
quite happy to believe

01:08:28.840 --> 01:08:31.060
that if one of those scuds
with chemical weapons,

01:08:31.060 --> 01:08:34.510
for a variety of deeply-held
historical and religious

01:08:34.510 --> 01:08:37.300
reasons, had been
sent into Tel Aviv

01:08:37.300 --> 01:08:40.029
I believe we might well have
seen a nuclear weapon popped

01:08:40.029 --> 01:08:43.899
into Baghdad, without
much question.

01:08:43.899 --> 01:08:46.660
So in that sense I think
you're absolutely right.

01:08:46.660 --> 01:08:51.340
The issue about
how do we help or--

01:08:51.340 --> 01:08:52.700
I'm not sure we can do a lot--

01:08:52.700 --> 01:08:57.399
but how do we understand
the dynamics of deterrence

01:08:57.399 --> 01:08:59.420
within the unstable
regions of the world?

01:08:59.420 --> 01:09:00.970
They may not have anything--

01:09:00.970 --> 01:09:03.090
Doesn't involve
Washington at all.

01:09:03.090 --> 01:09:05.680
How can we understand the
dynamics between Israel--

01:09:05.680 --> 01:09:07.580
which we, I think,
much too easily

01:09:07.580 --> 01:09:11.229
have permitted to
become a nuclear power--

01:09:11.229 --> 01:09:13.340
work that deterrence
within those regions

01:09:13.340 --> 01:09:16.790
is one of those
questions which I think

01:09:16.790 --> 01:09:19.470
plagues political leaders
throughout the globe

01:09:19.470 --> 01:09:23.505
and to which there
are no good answers.

01:09:23.505 --> 01:09:26.170
MODERATOR: [INAUDIBLE] We
have a second questioner there

01:09:26.170 --> 01:09:27.853
with a brief question.

01:09:27.853 --> 01:09:30.890
AUDIENCE: [INAUDIBLE] positions
of social responsibility.

01:09:30.890 --> 01:09:35.770
I wanted to come to Dr. Deutch's
point about how to deter.

01:09:35.770 --> 01:09:39.790
And I wonder here the idea of
setting an example is useful?

01:09:39.790 --> 01:09:42.724
And there are several examples
in history-- one, of course,

01:09:42.724 --> 01:09:43.970
is that of slavery--

01:09:43.970 --> 01:09:46.120
that some institutions
do, in fact,

01:09:46.120 --> 01:09:49.272
weather it over time, even
without force being [INAUDIBLE]

01:09:49.272 --> 01:09:50.660
against them.

01:09:50.660 --> 01:09:52.545
Another example,
perhaps, is that

01:09:52.545 --> 01:09:56.930
of nuclear weapons testing
above ground which, in fact,

01:09:56.930 --> 01:09:59.144
there was a treaty.

01:09:59.144 --> 01:10:01.330
But that treaty has
been enforced probably

01:10:01.330 --> 01:10:03.976
largely by virtue of the
voluntary participation

01:10:03.976 --> 01:10:06.843
of the nations who
have nuclear weapons

01:10:06.843 --> 01:10:08.717
and could have continue
above-ground testing.

01:10:08.717 --> 01:10:12.780
And in fact we see that even
non-signatories in the '70s

01:10:12.780 --> 01:10:16.409
and '80s stopped above-ground
testing and gradually also

01:10:16.409 --> 01:10:17.617
stopping underground testing.

01:10:17.617 --> 01:10:21.270
So I'm not sure if we have to
throw up our hands in despair

01:10:21.270 --> 01:10:24.060
and say there is no
way of thinking of how

01:10:24.060 --> 01:10:25.300
to deter this development.

01:10:25.300 --> 01:10:29.800
Maybe one way we could think
of is by setting an example,

01:10:29.800 --> 01:10:32.070
and having the United
States do that.

01:10:32.070 --> 01:10:34.410
JOHN DEUTCH: Well-- if I could
briefly respond to that--

01:10:34.410 --> 01:10:38.250
I don't think that there
is a very serious case that

01:10:38.250 --> 01:10:41.550
could be made to say what
the United States does

01:10:41.550 --> 01:10:45.570
with its nuclear weapons
program will influence

01:10:45.570 --> 01:10:50.820
what Iraq, Libya, North
Korea, or China do

01:10:50.820 --> 01:10:52.290
with their programs.

01:10:52.290 --> 01:10:54.240
Most observers, I think--

01:10:54.240 --> 01:10:56.880
serious observers of
non-proliferation--

01:10:56.880 --> 01:11:03.240
certainly encounter, in
diplomatic negotiations,

01:11:03.240 --> 01:11:05.880
the notion that if you
do as an example give up

01:11:05.880 --> 01:11:09.060
all your nuclear weapons, give
up all your nuclear tests,

01:11:09.060 --> 01:11:11.130
that that will help us do it.

01:11:11.130 --> 01:11:16.350
India, being a prominent nation
which makes such arguments.

01:11:16.350 --> 01:11:18.720
I think it's really
not really credible.

01:11:18.720 --> 01:11:21.540
What is driving these nations
to acquire their weapons

01:11:21.540 --> 01:11:24.150
capability is not the
presence of the United States,

01:11:24.150 --> 01:11:27.830
but the presence of the regional
interests that they have.

01:11:27.830 --> 01:11:30.330
And indeed I'm personally not
convinced that the world would

01:11:30.330 --> 01:11:33.120
be a better place if
the US had zero weapons

01:11:33.120 --> 01:11:36.490
and other nations had a few.

01:11:36.490 --> 01:11:38.424
MODERATOR: A third
question there.

01:11:38.424 --> 01:11:44.820
AUDIENCE: [INAUDIBLE]
physicist at MIT.

01:11:44.820 --> 01:11:50.770
Can you say a few words about
the problems, that you didn't

01:11:50.770 --> 01:11:55.230
touch at all upon, on the
possible use of such weapons

01:11:55.230 --> 01:11:59.230
by clandestine, non-governmental
terrorism groups?

01:12:02.022 --> 01:12:03.730
MATTHEW MESELSON:
Whose question is that?

01:12:03.730 --> 01:12:05.650
MODERATOR: Who ever
feels like answering.

01:12:05.650 --> 01:12:06.560
MATTHEW MESELSON:
Is that for me?

01:12:06.560 --> 01:12:07.130
JOHN DEUTCH: That's for you.

01:12:07.130 --> 01:12:07.900
MODERATOR: Consider it yours.

01:12:07.900 --> 01:12:10.100
MATTHEW MESELSON: Well,
it's always possible.

01:12:10.100 --> 01:12:12.900
There are lots of things to
worry about in the world.

01:12:12.900 --> 01:12:15.670
I've never thought of that as
being at the top of the list.

01:12:15.670 --> 01:12:18.650
But terrorists don't get
their weapons from nowhere.

01:12:18.650 --> 01:12:23.480
Usually it is some organized
entity which develops a weapon,

01:12:23.480 --> 01:12:26.680
tests it, produces
it, stockpiles it,

01:12:26.680 --> 01:12:30.860
and then eventually
maybe it finds its way

01:12:30.860 --> 01:12:33.800
into the public domain and
then terrorists can use it.

01:12:33.800 --> 01:12:36.830
So far the record on terrorists
has been relatively empty,

01:12:36.830 --> 01:12:38.930
if you mean biological
and chemical weapons.

01:12:41.540 --> 01:12:44.720
First of all, you could
worry about mad men.

01:12:44.720 --> 01:12:47.750
Mad people generally don't
cooperate with each other,

01:12:47.750 --> 01:12:49.697
so individual--

01:12:49.697 --> 01:12:51.905
to do most of these things
requires some cooperation.

01:12:51.905 --> 01:12:52.405
[CHUCKLES]

01:12:52.405 --> 01:12:55.610
So perhaps that's why
we don't see that.

01:12:55.610 --> 01:13:00.170
Organized terrorists, who
aren't individual mad people,

01:13:00.170 --> 01:13:02.610
usually have a political agenda.

01:13:02.610 --> 01:13:04.317
This is certainly not my field.

01:13:04.317 --> 01:13:06.650
I shouldn't be sounding like
any kind of expert on this,

01:13:06.650 --> 01:13:09.440
but I would say
that they usually

01:13:09.440 --> 01:13:11.360
have a political agenda.

01:13:11.360 --> 01:13:14.690
And simply causing fear
and terror and destruction

01:13:14.690 --> 01:13:17.630
doesn't match up with most
terrorist groups' agendas.

01:13:17.630 --> 01:13:21.620
They want to obtain the
sympathy of somebody.

01:13:21.620 --> 01:13:24.230
And so long as there is
a worldwide abhorrence

01:13:24.230 --> 01:13:27.260
of those weapons, they risk
losing the sympathy even

01:13:27.260 --> 01:13:32.430
of the group that
they wish to get

01:13:32.430 --> 01:13:34.539
to do something that they want.

01:13:34.539 --> 01:13:36.330
So I don't know if this
really works, but I

01:13:36.330 --> 01:13:39.300
would come to the
conclusion that maintaining

01:13:39.300 --> 01:13:44.190
a species-wide taboo against
certain kinds of weapons

01:13:44.190 --> 01:13:46.860
can only help keep
terrorists who

01:13:46.860 --> 01:13:50.370
have a political agenda
from turning to such weapons

01:13:50.370 --> 01:13:53.460
as tools for achieving
their political objectives.

01:13:53.460 --> 01:13:57.280
But other than that, I would
like to see a couple of things.

01:13:57.280 --> 01:13:59.760
First of all, one thing
John said about the treaty

01:13:59.760 --> 01:14:00.900
I certainly agree with.

01:14:00.900 --> 01:14:04.260
Its verification
provisions are imperfect.

01:14:04.260 --> 01:14:07.630
It will add a new tool,
and a valuable tool,

01:14:07.630 --> 01:14:12.435
so we're glad to have it because
we won't make as many type

01:14:12.435 --> 01:14:15.480
Iraq-type mistakes in the area
of chemicals with the treaty

01:14:15.480 --> 01:14:17.430
as we would without
them, unless it leads us

01:14:17.430 --> 01:14:18.840
into overconfidence.

01:14:18.840 --> 01:14:20.580
But we should beware of that.

01:14:20.580 --> 01:14:23.510
But it is a new tool.

01:14:23.510 --> 01:14:25.320
There is a debate
as to whether or not

01:14:25.320 --> 01:14:30.090
the United States ought to have
backed away from its original

01:14:30.090 --> 01:14:34.280
1986 policy of wanting to have
what's called "anytime anywhere

01:14:34.280 --> 01:14:37.090
challenge inspection" under the
Chemical Weapons Convention.

01:14:37.090 --> 01:14:41.010
And there are two sides to this,
but basically the United States

01:14:41.010 --> 01:14:45.220
does not want to let any of
its black programs be captured.

01:14:45.220 --> 01:14:47.520
So we want to retain the
right to be able to say,

01:14:47.520 --> 01:14:48.619
you can't go there.

01:14:48.619 --> 01:14:50.910
We'll try to convince you
some other way that we're not

01:14:50.910 --> 01:14:53.990
making chemical weapons there.

01:14:53.990 --> 01:14:57.560
It even goes so far that whereas
the United States Chemical

01:14:57.560 --> 01:15:01.370
Manufacturers Association has
said that, so far as they're

01:15:01.370 --> 01:15:04.550
concerned, they would be
willing to have all chemical

01:15:04.550 --> 01:15:06.890
facilities, whether
they make beer

01:15:06.890 --> 01:15:10.130
or whether they make plastics
or whatever they're making,

01:15:10.130 --> 01:15:14.540
be subject to open access
so long as certain kinds

01:15:14.540 --> 01:15:16.550
of protections for
proprietary information--

01:15:16.550 --> 01:15:19.400
but open access
for sample-taking

01:15:19.400 --> 01:15:23.090
and negative sample analysis
is OK with the Chemical

01:15:23.090 --> 01:15:24.859
Manufacturers Association.

01:15:24.859 --> 01:15:26.900
But it's not OK with the
United States government

01:15:26.900 --> 01:15:29.480
because there may be a few
industrial establishments where

01:15:29.480 --> 01:15:30.975
there are black programs.

01:15:30.975 --> 01:15:32.600
And you can debate
this back and forth.

01:15:32.600 --> 01:15:34.224
And maybe there are
other countries who

01:15:34.224 --> 01:15:36.740
wouldn't allow the "anytime
anywhere challenge."

01:15:36.740 --> 01:15:39.620
and we won't get them on board
with it even if we stick to it.

01:15:39.620 --> 01:15:42.800
I myself would rather see
more-- as much as possible--

01:15:42.800 --> 01:15:47.510
anytime anywhere,
and I would ask--

01:15:47.510 --> 01:15:49.827
rhetorically, anyway--
a black program

01:15:49.827 --> 01:15:51.410
is usually intended
to make something,

01:15:51.410 --> 01:15:53.420
like a weapon or a defensive
thing or something.

01:15:53.420 --> 01:15:55.845
So you could ask, let's say
we could have that thing

01:15:55.845 --> 01:15:57.470
and it's going to
work, whatever it is.

01:15:57.470 --> 01:15:59.375
Glasses that can see
into people's minds,

01:15:59.375 --> 01:16:00.250
or whatever you want.

01:16:00.250 --> 01:16:01.970
[LAUGHTER]

01:16:01.970 --> 01:16:05.630
But if instead I can also
increase the capability which,

01:16:05.630 --> 01:16:10.010
as John says, is not
too good for finding out

01:16:10.010 --> 01:16:14.050
what other people are up to,
maybe that trade off is worth

01:16:14.050 --> 01:16:14.590
it.

01:16:14.590 --> 01:16:16.760
So it should be
looked at that way.

01:16:16.760 --> 01:16:19.420
So that is still an
issue with the treaty.

01:16:19.420 --> 01:16:21.400
But I think that either
way it's something

01:16:21.400 --> 01:16:23.170
that can be fixed later.

01:16:23.170 --> 01:16:25.480
We can make the
inspection better.

01:16:25.480 --> 01:16:28.060
It's going to be a whole lot
better than what exists today.

01:16:28.060 --> 01:16:30.390
And we can make it even better.

01:16:30.390 --> 01:16:32.170
The thing about the
"no first use"--

01:16:32.170 --> 01:16:34.330
that is now in the treaty.

01:16:34.330 --> 01:16:37.780
Article 1 now says that under
no circumstances first second

01:16:37.780 --> 01:16:41.830
or nth, all parties
renounce all use forever

01:16:41.830 --> 01:16:43.810
of chemical weapons.

01:16:43.810 --> 01:16:44.470
So that's good.

01:16:44.470 --> 01:16:47.760
And it was missing, and
it's there now, finally.

01:16:47.760 --> 01:16:49.980
As far as sanctions,
it's not there.

01:16:49.980 --> 01:16:52.080
There is a kind of empty
article where sanctions

01:16:52.080 --> 01:16:54.420
are supposed to get plugged
in in the bargaining that

01:16:54.420 --> 01:16:57.570
will go on in the last few
weeks of the negotiations.

01:16:57.570 --> 01:17:00.840
And there's a lot of talk about
them, and it's a long subject

01:17:00.840 --> 01:17:03.210
and there probably will be some.

01:17:03.210 --> 01:17:05.220
But they basically
will depend on what

01:17:05.220 --> 01:17:08.430
the UN special commission
in Iraq depends on.

01:17:08.430 --> 01:17:11.130
The reason that they were able
to stay in that parking lot

01:17:11.130 --> 01:17:12.810
with their documents--

01:17:12.810 --> 01:17:15.330
you know, the reason we found
those documents was probably

01:17:15.330 --> 01:17:17.580
because nations make mistakes.

01:17:17.580 --> 01:17:20.310
The Iraqis apparently thought
that all the cases which said,

01:17:20.310 --> 01:17:23.551
top secret, don't look at it,
in the basement weren't there.

01:17:23.551 --> 01:17:25.300
And when the special
commission got there,

01:17:25.300 --> 01:17:27.049
there in the basement
were all these cases

01:17:27.049 --> 01:17:29.580
full of top secret microfilms.

01:17:29.580 --> 01:17:31.170
And the first time
they took them all

01:17:31.170 --> 01:17:33.180
and put them in the
truck in the parking lot

01:17:33.180 --> 01:17:35.220
so the Iraqis came
and took it away.

01:17:35.220 --> 01:17:36.630
They got it back, some of it.

01:17:36.630 --> 01:17:39.300
But the next day, after they
kept on taking this stuff,

01:17:39.300 --> 01:17:42.030
they distributed a little
bit in everybody's clothing

01:17:42.030 --> 01:17:44.670
so that by direct
television link

01:17:44.670 --> 01:17:47.460
they were able to say to the
Iraqis, OK, if you take it

01:17:47.460 --> 01:17:50.460
we are under instructions
not to resist violently,

01:17:50.460 --> 01:17:53.730
but you'll be seen taking these
things from all of our clothing

01:17:53.730 --> 01:17:56.880
everywhere, all 40 of us,
and the Security Council

01:17:56.880 --> 01:17:58.590
will know this with
the speed of light

01:17:58.590 --> 01:18:00.630
because we are direct link.

01:18:00.630 --> 01:18:04.230
My point is that, if there
is a Security Council that

01:18:04.230 --> 01:18:06.360
backs these things
up-- especially

01:18:06.360 --> 01:18:09.300
if the permanent members
of the Security Council

01:18:09.300 --> 01:18:12.000
want a new world order-- there
will be a new world order.

01:18:12.000 --> 01:18:14.490
And if a very powerful
nation doesn't want it,

01:18:14.490 --> 01:18:15.820
it won't happen.

01:18:15.820 --> 01:18:19.890
So it will depend very
sensitively on what the most

01:18:19.890 --> 01:18:22.240
important nations want to do.

01:18:22.240 --> 01:18:26.120
And finally, about the
developing world and chemical

01:18:26.120 --> 01:18:29.720
weapons, the advantages
of the treaty--

01:18:29.720 --> 01:18:32.330
if I were talking to a
purely self-interested

01:18:32.330 --> 01:18:34.700
American audience--
it's very simple.

01:18:34.700 --> 01:18:36.884
In this world today there
is high-tech weapons

01:18:36.884 --> 01:18:38.300
and there's very
low-tech weapons,

01:18:38.300 --> 01:18:40.790
and there's all kinds
of stuff in between.

01:18:40.790 --> 01:18:42.290
The thing about
high-tech weapons

01:18:42.290 --> 01:18:45.080
is that we're the only ones,
and a few other industrialized

01:18:45.080 --> 01:18:46.655
nations, that can afford them.

01:18:46.655 --> 01:18:48.030
There are lots
and lots of people

01:18:48.030 --> 01:18:49.700
who can afford the
low-tech weapons.

01:18:49.700 --> 01:18:52.400
So naturally it's
only common sense

01:18:52.400 --> 01:18:54.950
to say let's discredit
low-tech weapons.

01:18:54.950 --> 01:18:56.630
Let's de-legitimize them.

01:18:56.630 --> 01:18:57.630
Let's prohibit them.

01:18:57.630 --> 01:18:58.546
Let's get rid of them.

01:18:58.546 --> 01:19:01.580
And then nobody will be
able to have war except us.

01:19:01.580 --> 01:19:03.372
And maybe even nobody
would be even better.

01:19:03.372 --> 01:19:03.871
But--

01:19:03.871 --> 01:19:04.550
[LAUGHTER]

01:19:04.550 --> 01:19:05.430
I'm not joking.

01:19:05.430 --> 01:19:07.250
That's a serious
self-interested argument.

01:19:07.250 --> 01:19:10.641
Why have more players
who can do bad things?

01:19:10.641 --> 01:19:12.140
On the other hand,
this doesn't mean

01:19:12.140 --> 01:19:14.960
that a developing country should
be opposed to the convention.

01:19:14.960 --> 01:19:18.350
Because who are the victims
of chemical weapons usually?

01:19:18.350 --> 01:19:19.490
Poor countries.

01:19:19.490 --> 01:19:20.780
Time after time.

01:19:20.780 --> 01:19:22.700
Every time-- there's
never been a case

01:19:22.700 --> 01:19:25.430
in which chemical weapons have
been used against forces who

01:19:25.430 --> 01:19:27.740
initially had any gas masks.

01:19:27.740 --> 01:19:29.420
In other words,
they're used against

01:19:29.420 --> 01:19:31.340
poor, unsophisticated forces.

01:19:31.340 --> 01:19:33.740
And usually the
threat today will

01:19:33.740 --> 01:19:37.560
be from other poor, relatively
unsophisticated forces.

01:19:37.560 --> 01:19:39.025
So for them it's
a good deal too.

01:19:39.025 --> 01:19:40.400
So that's why I
think we're going

01:19:40.400 --> 01:19:41.850
to get the chemical treaty.

01:19:41.850 --> 01:19:44.360
Even India and
Pakistan, impatient

01:19:44.360 --> 01:19:46.000
to see the chemical
treaty come along,

01:19:46.000 --> 01:19:48.620
are now having bilateral
talks about having

01:19:48.620 --> 01:19:52.980
their own verification,
et cetera, on chemicals.

01:19:52.980 --> 01:19:55.760
So I think we're
going to get it.

01:19:55.760 --> 01:19:59.830
I do think that one great
pity would be if it allows--

01:19:59.830 --> 01:20:01.370
and it's not just
tear gas we want.

01:20:01.370 --> 01:20:04.880
We want to roll that
way back, in caps.

01:20:04.880 --> 01:20:06.050
And I agree with John.

01:20:06.050 --> 01:20:07.535
It's military peanuts.

01:20:07.535 --> 01:20:10.310
And to open the door
to what could possibly

01:20:10.310 --> 01:20:13.430
be dragged through that
door by insisting on that

01:20:13.430 --> 01:20:14.680
could be a big mistake.

01:20:14.680 --> 01:20:15.430
JOHN DEUTCH: Yeah.

01:20:15.430 --> 01:20:17.589
Am I allowed to make
a couple observations?

01:20:17.589 --> 01:20:19.130
MODERATOR: I'd be
a fool to stop you.

01:20:19.130 --> 01:20:19.590
JOHN DEUTCH: No, no.

01:20:19.590 --> 01:20:20.600
You could [INAUDIBLE].

01:20:20.600 --> 01:20:21.430
[CHUCKLES]

01:20:21.430 --> 01:20:24.307
This goes back to the issue
of whether mad men cooperate.

01:20:24.307 --> 01:20:26.175
[LAUGHTER]

01:20:28.200 --> 01:20:30.449
MODERATOR: An expert
on this subject.

01:20:30.449 --> 01:20:32.240
JOHN DEUTCH: The first
point I want to make

01:20:32.240 --> 01:20:37.940
is that I do think that
the inspection issue really

01:20:37.940 --> 01:20:41.570
has to be looked at
kind of carefully.

01:20:41.570 --> 01:20:44.285
I mean, the Chemical
Manufacturers Associate

01:20:44.285 --> 01:20:45.980
of the United States--

01:20:45.980 --> 01:20:47.780
some of my best friends--

01:20:47.780 --> 01:20:49.120
[LAUGHTER]

01:20:49.120 --> 01:20:50.870
The moment that they
saw that this was not

01:20:50.870 --> 01:20:54.604
a device for EPA to get
measurements on their plants--

01:20:54.604 --> 01:20:56.460
[LAUGHTER]

01:20:56.460 --> 01:20:59.080
--also realized
that it was not--

01:20:59.080 --> 01:20:59.989
that's a serious--

01:20:59.989 --> 01:21:01.280
I mean, I'm serious about that.

01:21:01.280 --> 01:21:03.050
You could imagine
such information

01:21:03.050 --> 01:21:05.660
being used for a lot of purposes
that would distress them.

01:21:05.660 --> 01:21:07.130
I think they really--

01:21:07.130 --> 01:21:08.840
you know, the
chemical manufacturers

01:21:08.840 --> 01:21:10.850
of Germany, France,
England, the United States--

01:21:10.850 --> 01:21:14.090
they're just not going to have
a problem with these kinds

01:21:14.090 --> 01:21:16.340
of inspections.

01:21:16.340 --> 01:21:20.840
And on the other hand, going
in to a modern chemical plant

01:21:20.840 --> 01:21:25.140
and inspecting it on a
challenge basis is a big deal.

01:21:25.140 --> 01:21:26.930
And you really
have to go through

01:21:26.930 --> 01:21:29.210
with some amusement, some
of the test inspections,

01:21:29.210 --> 01:21:30.560
to get a feeling.

01:21:30.560 --> 01:21:32.810
You go to one of these places--

01:21:32.810 --> 01:21:35.930
Bayou Choctaw,
Louisiana-- and try and do

01:21:35.930 --> 01:21:38.540
an inspection on a plan,
it is a major problem.

01:21:38.540 --> 01:21:42.210
I mean, it requires
more than just cadres

01:21:42.210 --> 01:21:44.250
of chemical engineers
and analytical chemists.

01:21:44.250 --> 01:21:45.210
It's a lot of work.

01:21:45.210 --> 01:21:47.400
So it's not so easy to do.

01:21:47.400 --> 01:21:49.050
And everything
has to work right.

01:21:49.050 --> 01:21:51.280
Things don't work
right, big deal.

01:21:51.280 --> 01:21:55.219
The matter of a covert is
really where I have a problem.

01:21:55.219 --> 01:21:56.010
And it's not that--

01:21:56.010 --> 01:21:57.990
I mean, I agree with Matt.

01:21:57.990 --> 01:22:00.750
I think the world, the
United States and the world

01:22:00.750 --> 01:22:03.230
is better off having
this treaty than not.

01:22:03.230 --> 01:22:07.320
But in a covert inspection
you have what you believe--

01:22:07.320 --> 01:22:10.110
your policy system has
led you to believe,

01:22:10.110 --> 01:22:13.430
is an accurate picture.

01:22:13.430 --> 01:22:15.650
Note that the United
States doesn't always

01:22:15.650 --> 01:22:18.830
have accurate pictures.

01:22:18.830 --> 01:22:20.760
Even in areas
where I advise them

01:22:20.760 --> 01:22:22.760
it doesn't have
accurate pictures.

01:22:22.760 --> 01:22:23.760
[LAUGHTER]

01:22:23.760 --> 01:22:28.662
And now, in order to
actually tell a country--

01:22:28.662 --> 01:22:30.370
and, by the way, we
want to come in there

01:22:30.370 --> 01:22:32.260
and we're going
to make it stick.

01:22:32.260 --> 01:22:35.980
That is big political
news, and I see no evidence

01:22:35.980 --> 01:22:38.050
that the world is
prepared to do that.

01:22:38.050 --> 01:22:39.670
The concert of nations.

01:22:39.670 --> 01:22:42.390
Let's take the example given.

01:22:42.390 --> 01:22:48.290
India and Pakistan are now in
an absolute frenzy of affection

01:22:48.290 --> 01:22:51.290
on the issue of
chemical weapons treaty.

01:22:51.290 --> 01:22:53.630
But if you want to talk
to them about inspecting

01:22:53.630 --> 01:22:57.610
nuclear facilities,
especially the Pakistanis,

01:22:57.610 --> 01:23:01.310
you will find that they
are quite unwilling to have

01:23:01.310 --> 01:23:03.710
nuclear inspectors, even
International Atomic

01:23:03.710 --> 01:23:06.869
Energy much less people--

01:23:06.869 --> 01:23:08.160
they aren't willing to have it.

01:23:08.160 --> 01:23:12.100
And if you try and make
it a political issue,

01:23:12.100 --> 01:23:13.930
the first people who
will say "back off"

01:23:13.930 --> 01:23:15.610
is our own State
Department because they

01:23:15.610 --> 01:23:18.190
say we have many too many
other important issues to deal

01:23:18.190 --> 01:23:20.980
with with respect to
Pakistan, than to blow it

01:23:20.980 --> 01:23:24.160
all on such a minor matter
as a proliferation of weapons

01:23:24.160 --> 01:23:25.000
of mass destruction.

01:23:25.000 --> 01:23:25.499
[LAUGHTER]

01:23:25.499 --> 01:23:28.360
So the issue of inspection--

01:23:28.360 --> 01:23:30.910
so in the Carter
administration--

01:23:30.910 --> 01:23:36.980
it was the Carter
administration who backed off

01:23:36.980 --> 01:23:40.790
from making Pakistan own up
to its enrichment facilities,

01:23:40.790 --> 01:23:43.864
because they felt
that having Pakistan

01:23:43.864 --> 01:23:45.530
favorable to the
United States' position

01:23:45.530 --> 01:23:48.710
in the matter of Afghanistan
was more important than to make

01:23:48.710 --> 01:23:51.020
an issue of enrichment.

01:23:51.020 --> 01:23:53.785
That happened in the
Carter administration.

01:23:53.785 --> 01:23:55.910
So the point I want to make
is, with the inspection

01:23:55.910 --> 01:23:57.620
it's not just saying--

01:23:57.620 --> 01:24:00.510
and I'm just reinforcing what
Matt has said-- it's not just

01:24:00.510 --> 01:24:02.870
saying, "anytime, anyplace."

01:24:02.870 --> 01:24:07.400
It's being sure that you
have the political depth,

01:24:07.400 --> 01:24:10.730
domestically and
internationally, to actually do

01:24:10.730 --> 01:24:13.276
something about it
to make it stick.

01:24:13.276 --> 01:24:14.900
MODERATOR: We'll take
one last question

01:24:14.900 --> 01:24:17.274
that I've been promised will
be very brief, because we're

01:24:17.274 --> 01:24:20.244
right about the time
we'll need close.

01:24:20.244 --> 01:24:21.090
The question.

01:24:21.090 --> 01:24:22.496
AUDIENCE: [INAUDIBLE]

01:24:22.496 --> 01:24:24.209
MODERATOR: Oh, Sure.

01:24:24.209 --> 01:24:24.750
AUDIENCE: Hi.

01:24:24.750 --> 01:24:25.715
I'm Jonathan King.

01:24:25.715 --> 01:24:28.040
I'm in the biology
department here.

01:24:28.040 --> 01:24:31.550
I had the privilege to serve
on the committee that organized

01:24:31.550 --> 01:24:34.070
the technical briefing for
the third review conference

01:24:34.070 --> 01:24:35.660
in Geneva.

01:24:35.660 --> 01:24:38.810
And I'd just like
to share with you--

01:24:38.810 --> 01:24:41.990
many of you may be interested
in actually continuing

01:24:41.990 --> 01:24:45.660
to ensure that the
treaty stays enforced

01:24:45.660 --> 01:24:47.505
and it remains effective.

01:24:47.505 --> 01:24:48.380
There are gray areas.

01:24:48.380 --> 01:24:50.330
The government does have
two funded programs--

01:24:50.330 --> 01:24:53.300
the BDRP, the Biological
Defense Research Program,

01:24:53.300 --> 01:24:56.240
with many, many, many contracts
all around the United States,

01:24:56.240 --> 01:24:58.100
many in the Boston area.

01:24:58.100 --> 01:25:01.940
And Congress has authorized the
BATF, the Biological Aerosol

01:25:01.940 --> 01:25:04.250
Test Facility in Dugway, Utah.

01:25:04.250 --> 01:25:06.740
At this very moment
hundreds of microbiologists,

01:25:06.740 --> 01:25:09.770
faculty at University
of Utah Medical School,

01:25:09.770 --> 01:25:11.930
University of Utah
itself, are still

01:25:11.930 --> 01:25:16.190
engaged in dueling with
the army over whether that

01:25:16.190 --> 01:25:17.140
should develop.

01:25:17.140 --> 01:25:19.880
And the good features
that Matt has developed

01:25:19.880 --> 01:25:24.290
have depended upon quiet but
constant pressure on Congress

01:25:24.290 --> 01:25:27.700
from members of primarily
the biomedical community.

01:25:27.700 --> 01:25:29.720
The Physicians for
Social Responsibility

01:25:29.720 --> 01:25:34.120
nationally have a conscious
monitoring program of BW.

01:25:34.120 --> 01:25:38.000
The Federation of American
Scientists, which Matt--

01:25:38.000 --> 01:25:42.060
you're not chairing--
played a key role in, had

01:25:42.060 --> 01:25:43.880
an active committee.

01:25:43.880 --> 01:25:47.947
And I worked with a group of
more than 1,000 scientists

01:25:47.947 --> 01:25:50.030
who have signed a pledge
saying we're going to try

01:25:50.030 --> 01:25:52.700
to strengthen this treaty.

01:25:52.700 --> 01:25:56.540
And we watch what
our congressmen

01:25:56.540 --> 01:26:01.910
vote on these issues,
and let them know that--

01:26:01.910 --> 01:26:05.990
not just Matt and John, but
also average working scientists

01:26:05.990 --> 01:26:09.230
in their districts
competing with the military

01:26:09.230 --> 01:26:10.980
for biomedical research funds--

01:26:10.980 --> 01:26:12.920
do not think this
is a good thing.

01:26:12.920 --> 01:26:15.110
I just might mention that
that network is actually

01:26:15.110 --> 01:26:19.040
based in Cambridge at 19
Garden Street, the Council

01:26:19.040 --> 01:26:20.760
for Responsible Genetics.

01:26:20.760 --> 01:26:23.450
And if you want to join in
and receive regular updates

01:26:23.450 --> 01:26:26.150
and you want to let your--

01:26:26.150 --> 01:26:30.380
you want to be aware of what
our government is doing on this,

01:26:30.380 --> 01:26:31.694
we welcome you, and it's easy.

01:26:31.694 --> 01:26:33.110
And these are
professional issues.

01:26:33.110 --> 01:26:35.374
They should be in the
professional societies.

01:26:35.374 --> 01:26:37.040
Graduate students
should know that we're

01:26:37.040 --> 01:26:39.784
signatory to a treaty,
and that's a good treaty.

01:26:39.784 --> 01:26:42.200
It should be in the business
of the professional meetings.

01:26:42.200 --> 01:26:42.750
Thanks.

01:26:42.750 --> 01:26:43.430
MODERATOR: Thanks.

01:26:43.430 --> 01:26:45.980
Do either of our speakers want
to add something [INAUDIBLE]??

01:26:45.980 --> 01:26:47.840
MATTHEW MESELSON: Jonathan
mentioned some opportunities

01:26:47.840 --> 01:26:49.160
for people to do something.

01:26:49.160 --> 01:26:50.920
I was in Washington
last weekend.

01:26:50.920 --> 01:26:52.260
There is a program--

01:26:52.260 --> 01:26:54.170
in fact, most agencies
of the government--

01:26:54.170 --> 01:26:56.540
whereby faculty members,
junior or senior,

01:26:56.540 --> 01:27:00.110
who want to get involved in
these things in Washington

01:27:00.110 --> 01:27:02.900
for a year working for the
United States government--

01:27:02.900 --> 01:27:04.920
there's a program by
which you can do that.

01:27:04.920 --> 01:27:08.660
And in this particular area of
chemical and biological arms

01:27:08.660 --> 01:27:11.900
control, I knew of
several open positions.

01:27:11.900 --> 01:27:15.050
Also, people who have a
PhD who are interested

01:27:15.050 --> 01:27:17.840
not just in a year
away but in a career,

01:27:17.840 --> 01:27:19.810
I know of several
open positions.

01:27:19.810 --> 01:27:22.940
And it's a fascinating
series of problems,

01:27:22.940 --> 01:27:26.540
and there's probably no time
in our country's history

01:27:26.540 --> 01:27:28.280
when there's been
more opportunity

01:27:28.280 --> 01:27:29.930
for creative thinking
about how to deal

01:27:29.930 --> 01:27:31.500
with some of these problems.

01:27:31.500 --> 01:27:33.350
So if anybody's
really interested

01:27:33.350 --> 01:27:35.970
and doesn't know how to go
find those things themselves,

01:27:35.970 --> 01:27:37.292
I'd be glad to help you.

01:27:37.292 --> 01:27:39.000
MODERATOR: The hour
is later and it'll be

01:27:39.000 --> 01:27:40.710
time for us to close just now.

01:27:40.710 --> 01:27:45.080
I'd like to simply say that,
when Jerry fink and I began

01:27:45.080 --> 01:27:48.560
discussing the possibility of a
series like this some time ago,

01:27:48.560 --> 01:27:52.250
it was because there was a
sense perceived on our part that

01:27:52.250 --> 01:27:55.070
our colleagues on the
faculty, postdocs we knew,

01:27:55.070 --> 01:27:58.550
the graduate students all had an
interest-- a growing interest,

01:27:58.550 --> 01:27:59.310
in fact--

01:27:59.310 --> 01:28:02.630
in understanding and talking
about the social issues

01:28:02.630 --> 01:28:05.480
that related to our science
but was outside the laboratory.

01:28:05.480 --> 01:28:08.420
I'm very pleased tonight to
see so many people from so

01:28:08.420 --> 01:28:11.210
many different areas turn out
to confirm that that is indeed

01:28:11.210 --> 01:28:12.260
the case.

01:28:12.260 --> 01:28:17.090
The goal of this series, both
tonight and in future lectures,

01:28:17.090 --> 01:28:19.880
will not be to try to
provide answers, advocate

01:28:19.880 --> 01:28:22.850
any particular positions,
but really to try to squarely

01:28:22.850 --> 01:28:26.840
address hard questions, and
principally to be enlightening,

01:28:26.840 --> 01:28:29.960
to provide information so that
people can go on from here

01:28:29.960 --> 01:28:32.270
and think about things
and perhaps take

01:28:32.270 --> 01:28:34.040
some active role
in those things.

01:28:34.040 --> 01:28:35.850
Our next speaker will
be Paul Billings,

01:28:35.850 --> 01:28:37.640
speaking about genetic
discrimination.

01:28:37.640 --> 01:28:40.040
He's a speaker I know well
and I think will, again,

01:28:40.040 --> 01:28:40.850
contribute to that.

01:28:40.850 --> 01:28:43.990
But for tonight I want to
especially thank the two--

01:28:43.990 --> 01:28:47.030
our speaker and our discussant,
who've did a wonderful job

01:28:47.030 --> 01:28:50.030
of enlightening us about
this very complicated topic

01:28:50.030 --> 01:28:52.322
with their very sober and
very important comments.

01:28:52.322 --> 01:28:53.780
Thank you very much
for joining us.

01:28:53.780 --> 01:28:56.230
[APPLAUSE]

